A||||119||Broken_hearted||DIVORCE.... will I survive? (long)||||9||
Z||000000||Broken_hearted||05-15-2001||10:44 PM||||I am a long-time member of the forum, but I am using this new name to discuss this here with you "strangers". I guess I am too ashamed to let you know who I am.<P>In 1992 I met my wife. We hit it off in no time at all and were always together. We dated for almost 2 years before her parents found out we were living together. I always planned on marrying her, but her parents thought it was a good idea for us to get married very soon. We ended up getting married on May 14 1994 (yesterday was our 7th anniversary). I was nervous and felt her parents didn't like me much.<P>After a few years she commented she wanted kids, but I wasn't ready yet. We never really sat down and discussed it and I figured she was OK with it. Then one day I was mad at her, and life in general I guess, and told her I never wanted kids. I can't believe I told her that, but I thought she knew I wasn't serious, and I never told her I was sorry. BIG MISTAKE! I was never good at saying that, I just figured she knew I was.<P>Around the beginning of this year I noticed she was being very private with what she was doing on the computer. Once I walked by and she clicked on another window to hide who she was talking to. I asked her why she hid that and she just said that she wanted to see the window she brought up(she was playing a game). Then I noticed new email accounts from Yahoo. I got curious about this and started looking for clues. After trying to find something I didn't want to see I found her Yahoo profile stored in the computer. Under Martial status it said, "No Answer"!! I asked her about it and she told me that's just what it was set to and she hasn't changed it yet. I wanted to believe her, but I acted angry towards her for some time. I couldn't believe this might be happening. Comes to find out she found her highshcool boyfriend and they started talking. I think it started out innocent, but now it's very serious. One day I actually asked her if she had an internet boyfriend, she said no and then asked me if I was having an affair. I have never cheated on her or even come close to it. My marriage ment the world to me.<P>Now I know I wasn't the perfect husband, but I don't think i was terrible. I never drank, I have never hit her(would never think of doing that), never stayed out all night... etc. But I have made some mistakes, mostly saying things I didn't mean. <P>Then I finally told her that if she had found someone that could make hew happier I wouldn't stand in the way... my exact words. As I lay there(we were in bed) waiting to hear I had nothing to worry about... I hear silence instead. My heart sank and I jumped out of bed. I didn't know what to do. We started talking and she told me she loves him! They dated in highscool for a while I guess, but never got serious. She swears to this day that I was her first and only(sex) and I believe her. Now she wants to throw away a 7 year marriage to see if she will be happier with this guy. I found out as much as I can about him and it seems he is a punk street-racer. His profile on Yahoo tells me this. He likes to drag race (imports) and "Highway battle". I think he has told her he has lots of money and plans on buying a huge house soon. I found a webpage stored in the computer he must have showed her that cost like $300,000. This from a 27 year old guy that has an apartment and a room-mate? This guy lives in California and thay haven't met yet. Once she asked me if she could go there and see him to "see if there is anything there". I was furious and, of course, told her no. then one day I come home and she tells me that she got 2 days off work because they slowed down. She told me she was going to see him. The only reason she didn't go was because I took the car keys. Now she tells me this has nothing to do with him. It's because I told her I never wanted a baby, but I actuall told her I was ready before this all happened. I told her that I was ready to start a family. She also says it's because I buy things we don't need (Fiero) and I expected her to be my maid. I guess maybe I took her for granted a bit, but I work my butt off all day and I am beat when i get home, but I never thought of her as my "slave" as she puts it.<P>It took her about a month and a half to fianally tell me for sure that she wants a divorce after she finally told me what was going on. Now all she wants to do is talk about how we are going to split up all our stuff. She moved in with her parents a month after she let me know. we tried to "work it out" for about 10 days before she started growing farther away from me. We went out almost every night and I thought we were going to make it... I really did. We have so much to fight for and everything to lose. She changed almost over night it seems. <P>I am taking this very hard and don't know what to do or to think. I'm all alone now and fear I will be forever. I also want kids very bad now and I feel that might be gone as well. It's hard to keep my chin up when all I think about is her... my best friend and my reason for breathing. I cry all the time. She used to cry with me, but not anymore. Deep down I hope that it doesn't work out between them and she might return. Isn't that crazy? Or is that love? I fear I will never meet the right girl again. I fear everything right now...<P>I am wondering what I can do in this divorce. The papers haven't been filed yet(because of me) but she wants it done soon. Can I make her life miserable because she is leaving me for another guy? She doesn't want me to contest it.. should I? She said she wanted the house, but I talked her out of it. I am woried that if I contest this she may change her mind and try to get the house. Can she get alimony? Will I survive this? What the hell am I supposed to do now? My whole reason for being acts like she hates me and wants to be divorced so bad. I'll stop now and wait for any advise...<P>Thanks<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||FieroLisa||05-15-2001||11:02 PM||||I have no amazing words of advice to you, that will make anything magically better, but I wish I did. <BR>I have never been married, but I have been in love and then lost it all for a reason that just doesn't seem right. <BR>You told her that you wanted her to be happy, with whoever it may be, and if you want to stay true to your word, I would go ahead with the divorce- stalling and procrastinating won't make her come back, it will make her angry at you. I believe in fate, so if she comes back, it will be for the reason that you two are meant for each other. <BR>Someone told me this "Love yourself first, then other people will love you" or something to that extent.  I know first hand it's impossible to just put your head up and act like everythings just OK, when it's really not. People will tell you to just move on, and you'll want to yell at them and tell them it's just not that easy, cause its not!<BR>So anyway you got me all emotional now, and I don't know if I make much sense, so I'll shut up.. Good things come unexpectedly, so just look forward to the time when things will eventually get better- because they will.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Loki||05-15-2001||11:18 PM||||Even though I wasnt married, Im going through alot now myself..My GF of 3 years and the mother of my son decided one day it wasnt going to work between me and her (2 months ago) Was a shoke to me.. It hurts so bad. Thats a piece of my life gone form her. But like ive told myself for ever now (someday ill listen) Things happen for a reason.... It may not seem like it now. And maybe not for along time. But they happen for a reason higher than what ill ever understand. I am sorry to hear about this, but we here on the forum will always be here to listen and to help as best we can.  It may also be a phase she has to go through. Ive seen it before. As im hoping it is with my ex. But keep reminding yourself, its not the end. I tell myself is a re-beginning....<BR>  Good luck with evereyhting man, well be here if needed.....<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||RobertVol||05-15-2001||11:19 PM||||Man! Phew!<P>I am sorry to hear what you are going through. Anyone that has that happen to them needs a little support. I hope you have friends around you or things that can keep you busy through the tough times. One thing to think about is this....Children dont make a marriage there may have been more wrong than you think. The only reason I say that is many people get married and when they have problems, and think a child will solve them and it blows up in their face! IT usually causes more problems. Since this seems true, it would seem that logic would work in reverse. So Not having a baby should not stop you from having a happy marriage. I know from experience ....believe me. I was married for 9 yrs. There generally is more than meets the eye or problems underneath in the relationship. She may just not be happy, or she may need something different. Marriage is commitment that not to many people in this day and age really understand. If its just a piece of paper then why do things seem to go to crap after the license is signed and you are married? hmmmmmm<P>But anyway I hope everything works out and you get what you want , which is your marriage back I assume. But if the worse thing happens I still hope you can work it out and be a happier person and come out shining on the backside. GOOD LUCK!<P> ||||reg||2
Z||000004||Broken_hearted||05-15-2001||11:42 PM||||I seriously never saw this coming... until it was too late. I haven't cried since I was a kid, but now I cry myself to sleep and cry when I wake up in the empty bed. I'm tearing up now. I NEVER thought this would happen. She was always happy and smiling... if she wasn't happy, she hid it well.<P>I don't really have true friends anymore... she was my friend, my best friend. Not anyone I could talk to about this anyway. I'm hopeless. <P>I wasn't saying that I wanted a kid to keep her here, but she said that she wouldn't have done this if we had one/some. <P>If she would have left me because she wasn't happy, we would have lived a happy... forever. But she has another peson to go to now. I have noticed what I have done wrong and I truely changed. She says she is scared that I would change back (she has me thinking I am a monster) to my "old self". We never argued... well just little spats, you know the ones that are fun to make up for. We got along great and had many interests of the same things. I swear we were ment for eachother. Now I am alone with my memories.||||reg||1
Z||000005||beer_stud_21||05-16-2001||12:47 AM||||Hey man,  I'm no relationship expert or anything, but you've really put your feelings/thoughts down really well in these two posts.  If I were you and wanted this person to really know how I felt and what is going on I would e-mail her the URL to this thread.  Let her read it for herself so she can understand what has happened from your perspective.  Maybe she will realize where you two went wrong and see where how much she means to you.  Good luck<P>-Marlin<BR>ICQ# 8273989<P>btw: there's no need to hide behind a new anonymous identity.  I think I can speak for the fourm here when I say we would never think any less of you.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||05-16-2001||05:46 AM||||Just let me start by saying that what seems like the end of the world today, will be nothing more than a memory ten years from now. We have the incredible ability to bounce back from almost anything - if you give it enough time. Having to let the one you love go, is probably the worst thing to go through in your life. All you can do is pick up the pieces and move on.<P>I don't think trying to make your wife stay at this point will do any good. She obviously has made up her mind and anything you do to try to make her stay, will only strengthen her determination that she wants you out of her life. At this point you will have to <B>win</B> her back, not <B>force</B> her back. Of course you can make her life miserable and make the divorce hell - but is that really what you want? You'll destroy any respect she still has for you and with that any chance she'll ever get back to you.<P>The biggest mistake you can make right now is thinking she hasn't really made up her mind yet and that she isn't determined taking the path she has chosen. And that you can somehow influence her decisions. In my experience, by the time women tell you the thought has occurred to take their life in a certain direction, they actually have thought about it a lot and most probably already made a decision.<P>I'm hesitant to tell you what to do because every individual is different. So just to cover my own ass, take this exactly for what it is: a friendly advice and nothing more.<P>Let her go for now. It sounds like she has made up her mind and denying her the chance to find a better life (in her mind) will destroy any chance you have of ever winning her back. She thinks the road ahead of her is new and exiting and it is. But for the coming time, she will miss the comfort of having someone around she trusts and have experienced so much with. Be a friend. Be the one she will be secretly sending emails to. She's comfortable with communicating using computers, so give her your email address or better yet, your ICQ or AIM number. There will be times that she feels the need to talk with you so make that as easy as possible. Don't make the mistake that when she does contact you, it is a sign that she wants to come back. It's only a sign that she's not ready yet to totally give up on her old life and that's the only straw you have left so don't destroy it. She has to make her own decision as to what life she prefers. Don't overshadow any good old memories she has of her relationship with you with new bad ones.<P>I wouldn't show her this thread because she is trying to distance herself from what this might be doing to you. Giving her a feeling of guilt does not help your cause.<P>Anyway, to end this post the same way I started it with: whatever happens, eventually you'll bounce back. I've gone through something similar twice now. And my thought about that is, that if that never happened, I would not have met the girl I so dearly love and am engaged to now.<P>These are difficult times for you. Cry your eyes out, don't be afraid of your emotions. But remember that no matter how bad you feel now, in ten years it's nothing but a bad memory.<P>Take care.||||reg||1
Z||000007||mbramble||05-16-2001||08:10 AM||||You will survive!!<P>Sorry to hear about your problems.  Been there, though I wasn't married nearly that long (the first time!) I was still broken up when it didn't work out.  I was pretty sure that it was the end of the world for me, that I'd found my lifelong mate and then lost her.  But, as of April, I have been married for 19 years (damn!!) and am as happy now as I was 20 years ago when I met my REAL lifelong mate.  So, all isn't lost for you, yours is out there somewhere, too -- and maybe she'll love Fieros!!<P>I concur with Cliff about not making the mistake of thinking she hasn't already made up her mind.  <P>But, you need to see a lawyer.  Your questions about whether you should contest, who might get the house, alimony, etc., shouldn't be answered by any of us here and shouldn't be left up to some agreement you and her try and work out because either of you wants to get even or because either of you wants to be kind.  This is just not stuff that most people know how to deal with properly because, thankfully, we don't have to go thru it very often.  So, if divorce is inevitible, get some professional help on your side so that you come out of it as good as you can. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||05-16-2001||08:47 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroLisa:<BR><B>Someone told me this "Love yourself first, then other people will love you" or something to that extent.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Very good words to live by, I think everyone should adhere to that.  I gave the same advice to myself.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4 OD auto<BR>"Ride the geriatric mobile.. err..  rocket"||||reg||14
Z||000009||fierogt88||05-16-2001||09:17 AM||||I agree with everything said above, but I sure feel I should back up mbramble on the lawyer suggestion.  She is the one leaving you.  Even if you are the kind-hearted soul that would take her back when she figures out what a mistake she's making, you shouldn't let her take you down with her.  Go see that lawyer.  Keep your stuff.  Especially the house, since "he" is getting that big expensive one, she's got no use for yours.  If you let her have it, it would work against you since if he failed to be a provider for her, you will have already provided.  That doesn't leave much incentive for her to come to her senses.  Go see that lawyer.  From experience, I would suggest one that teaches on the side.  They know they're stuff and are very understanding of the younger generations.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||05-16-2001||09:27 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mbramble:<BR><B>You will survive!!<BR>&lt;snip&gt;<BR>But, you need to see a lawyer.  &lt;snip&gt;<BR>...if divorce is inevitible, get some professional help on your side so that you come out of it as good as you can. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I second this advice. <P>I know that you are hurting now. You will feel better with the passage of time. <BR>Sounds like she's made up her mind. Just let her do what she feels like she needs to do.  You can't stop her, and will probably alienate her even more if you try.<P>You do, however, need to look out for yourself and your own interests. It's quite clear that <I>she's</I> not. <BR>Get an attorney and cover your butt. <BR>You might even consider a countersuit.<P>Yeah. I know I sound cold. I'm really not. Just trying to be a realist. <P>The pain <I>will</I> pass. It will just take time. <P>Good luck.<P>Steve<BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||thomas_l||05-16-2001||09:46 AM||||Here here! I agree - you need to look after #1 right now. I divorced in '96 after 9 years. I can't say the circumstances were similar though. There wasn't an outside interest for either of us (that I knew of anyway). We had gone through some very difficult times financially and personally. After we hit the bottom, I came to realize that she was content to just deal with it (and not even try to improve our circumstance) while I was consistently working my ass off. When I finally quit rationalizing her careless actions and demanded 50/50 participation, she just wasn't interested, and in fact the lies and BS just got worse. When I finally reached the breaking point, and I am probably one of the most patient men you will meet, decision time (to end the marriage) was a real leap of faith. It truly was like stepping off a cliff with only my faith in myself that would get me through it.<P>You will see once the stress of splitting up has eased that she will get out of the relationship and want to come back to you for "safe sex" and "honey-do's". Mr. California (no offense to you other Californians  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) will be short term at best. Whatever money he may have is over-ridden by the apartment/roommate thing. He will not be able to offer the stability and level of comfort that you can provide. No gaurantee, but she will end up lonely (like you are) and engineer a way back.<P>Here's what I did. After the split, I definitely looked after #1. Nobody looks out for your interests and peace of mind like yourself. Take a few months to focus on important stuff like paying bills and working. Fill your free time with those real tedious, labor intesive Fiero projects you ahve been putting off. Next, when she comes knocking, and she will, more often than not you need to be unavailable. If you're too easy you will get used. Even if you stay home with the lights out to pretend you are out. If she really wants a new (&lt;-- don't miss this keyword) relationship with you, make it happen on your terms (look out for #1). My ex never changed and chose the easy path instead (found some other guy to lay up with).<P>Take advantage of the change in your life to get your self back on track. The first year or two were hell for me and working 60 hours a week and finishing up my college degree (GSU BBA '99!! I did it!) was where all that anger, sadness, lonliness, and other stuff got focused.<P>My story worked out pretty good in the end. Two years after I split with the ex (1yr after the official divorce - I did it myself, uncontested no kids, gave her whatever she wanted - let it go it's just stuff) I met a woman that has been my partner and best friend. We got married by Elvis in Vegas 10/21/00. I have put the past behind me, but have not forgotten the pain.<P>Good luck man!||||reg||14
Z||000012||Oreif||05-16-2001||10:53 AM||||I agree with everyone above. I have a lot of friends that have been divorced and can tell you if you want to retain some connection to her, Don't fight her on the divorce. As said before she has made up her mind. You cannot force her back. That would be a big mistake.<BR>My advise would be to seek a lawyer and cover yourself. In reality, she may go see the ex-boyfriend and realize the grass isn't always greener on the other side and return. It's obvious he spends alot of time/money on his cars. This is something she has to do herself. Nobody is going to be able tell her different. A few of the friends that divorced had simular experiences where one decided that it would be better to be single or see someone from the past. Usually it doesn't work out because what they expect the other person to give them isn't real. It is their idea of how it should be, and then the cold hard facts rear up and they are shocked by it. I have seen it numerous times. At that point is when they realize they didn't know what they had until it's  gone. The few who had simular experiences, by the time the one who wanted to leave realized what they lost, the other had gotten over it and found someone else. It is not a good idea for you to wait and see if she returns. This will just prolong the pain and isn't good for yourself. It is good to get your feelings out in the open (via the forum). This will help you get thru the pain and hopefully get you back on track.<BR> Finally, as hard as it may be, let her do her thing. We only learn from our mistakes. Once your pain has subsided, you can then get on with your life. There are many folks here who can give you support if you need it. Best thing about a forum this large, someone has had a simular experience and can help you get thru the difficult parts.<P>Take Care.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <BR>Got Emblems?||||reg||1
Z||000013||JSocha||05-16-2001||01:41 PM||||Understanding this is extremly hard for you, been there in the same boat...I think you were there fishing next to me...but take the advise of your "friends" given here.  Look out for yourself, definately "don't do anything stupid" cause it isn't worth it in the end and move on and try to remember the good times you shared and how you can improve on them in the future in another relationship (which will happen).<P>If you feel you've hit or are going to hit rock bottom, then don't let her or anyone else throw you a shovel.  Come here, let the emotions out as this is the best "sounding board" you will ever have.<P>Also, don't expect, anticipate, pray, etc. for her to come back even if she does and you feel there is hope in your relationship.  Just reading how you started this tread, there seems to be a lot of "trust" issues which have apparently been lost over time.  <P>With the loss of "trust" between you, you will always be wondering or suspicious, in the back of your mind, which will show to her and vise versa.  This is not healthy for either of you.<P>I'm also taking it you were very young when you got married.  Both of you may or may not have been mature enough to know what you really wanted in life and that is catching up now with her.<P>With that I will close as we can only share your pain that you feel but we can not feel it as you do.  We are here for you 24/7 so you can unload your emotional baggage!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||MotherGoose||05-16-2001||02:19 PM||||I completely understand.  My poor heart has been broken in a million pieces several times.  Twice I have had the misfortune in catching my girlfriend screwing another guy.  After the first one I didn't eat for a month except diet coke and crakers, I lost 65 pounds.  Another time I quite my job drove 2000 miles to south beach in Miami with three friends and spent $10000 dollars on booze and parties to forget her, didn't work.  I did the ceramonal things like writing her a letter and burning it, or writing in a journal.  The only thing that works is time.  I have said the same things you have and felt the same way.  But it's just not true.  Someone will love you again and you will love them.  In fact I just fell in love with a girl that ran by in a sports bra and tight shorts.  Not to make light, and I know a marrage is a little more than just a girl friend, but love is love.  When it ends in a way that we don't like it hurts.  Time is the only thing that will help, and maybe a friend.  Someone that will listen to you.  Girls are better at it than guys, but try to find one you aren't attracted to because that's a whole other problem you don't want.<P>That's all I got, it will just take time.  Don't drink to much, or lay around all day, and good luck it's a rough road a head.||||reg||1
Z||000015||deceler8||05-16-2001||04:11 PM||||You got screwed...now pick yourself up and move on.<P>Okay, maybe too short and to the point and perhaps unfeeling, but that's your mission, Jim, should you decide to accept it.<P>Reminds me of something Ken Titus (Stacy Keach) said on the fox show Titus...<P>"Why are Divorces so expensive...because they're worth every cent".||||reg||1
Z||000016||Loki||05-16-2001||04:46 PM||||Broken_Hearted..<BR>   PLease e-mail me or ICQ me..Im going through close to the same thing.....I cn help or atleast try my hardest<P>Loki-MG@home.com<BR>ICQ# 16477998<P><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroHarry||05-16-2001||06:09 PM||||Hey guy I just ended marraige number 2.<BR>Its never easy. But you will become sane again, I promise  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>It may seem like your world has ended but it really hasnt.<BR>Get a lawyer, dont fight her if you do it would ruin any chance of her ever changing her mind. Just cover your rear because if it gets ugly you could lose everything (ive seen it happen).<P><BR>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 263K+ <I>"TEMPTATION"</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B><p>[This message has been edited by FieroHarry (edited 05-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Old Lar||05-16-2001||07:59 PM||||Get a good lawyer to protect your interests.  Don't argue on what to split, lawyers work by the hour.  Not having children will be easier to make the break and will be less expensive.<P>My wife walked out after 8 years of marriage for Mr Wonderful who walked out on his wife and daughter.  I haven't heard from her since 1984, but Mr Wonderful walked out on her a few years later. I've heard she's working on #3 husband. What goes around, comes around.  <P>You will survive.||||reg||1
Z||000019||hugh||05-16-2001||08:12 PM||||I'm one of the lucky ones,I'm married to my best friend.You have to realize,as I have, that of the millions of women in this world there is more than one person you can be very happy with.The one you need and will want may live around the corner,or you may meet her tomorrow,or next month.BUT you will meet her and this period will be just a memory.Good luck in your pursuit of happiness. ||||reg||1
Z||000020||Broken_hearted||05-16-2001||09:19 PM||||Yesterday she left a note here for me: Sorry, but I still want a divorce. There is nothing you can do to make me stay. Please keep this friendly.<P>This is what I find the day after our 7th year of being married. I didn't call her or talk to her at all. And I deserve this?<P>I don't know how to tell you all this, but she was the sweetest thing before this happened. <P>Today I walk in to see another note: "I was going to stay and talk to you, but Jamie is having her baby. Maybe tomorrow". Jamie is her brother's wife. I know she wants to discuss getting the divorce going and not trying to work anything out. She is convinced that she will be better off with him... maybe she will be. I seriously don't want her to get hurt. This guy has changed since highschool I'm sure. But if he breaks her heart and she comes back to me... I would be so damn happy, but I would probably kill this guy. He has already been married and divorced. I asked her why they got divorced and she told me she didn't know. I guess he and his brother opened up a gun store and should make alot of money. We were never rich and had some financial trouble at times. She is a Travel Agent, but lost her job to the internet. She hasn't worked much in the last 3 years or so and that's mostly what caused the financial trouble. The fact that I am terrible with money doesn't help much either. Now she has a good job and money is rolling in, but she is gonna give that up too.<P>Now that I think back I can see why she wasn't happy... neither was I to an extent. I was always happy to come home to her, but something was missing. It was the magic of romance and the feeling of being "alive". We got that back when we were "trying to work it out" and it was like we first met again. We had sparks between us again, but a few days later she started to distance herself from me. She kept calling him during this period and she tells me she tried very hard to work it out between us. This is what has me so hurt... she gives me a reason to fight, then gives me no hope. Once I asked her why she was being so cold towards me and she said, "Don't take it personal." Then I said I take divroce personal and she told me she never said anything about divorce. What am I supposed to think? Through out all this I always asked her if she wanted me to give up and she would tell me not to. Then the day came that she left... I walked in and told her that we should stop all this nonsense and get on with our lives and start our family. We talked a bit and I guess it kinda got to her. She started packing her bags as I begged her to stay. The hardest thing I have ever done is carry out my wife's bags to her car so she could leave me. She finally left and I felt relief for some reason. I guess I thought she would miss me and realize she made a mistake. She called 2 days later telling me she missed me and wanted to see me. She said she would stay home the next day(she stops by here after work) until I got there. We talked a bit and I had hope again. Then a couple days later she started being cold towards me again. I know her parents support whatever she decides and I guess that gave her strength. They don't know about the other guy yet, but I plan to tell them. My wife doesn't want them to know because "they would think it was because of him".<P>Maybe I am telling you this so you will tell me that she is confused and will return to me, but I know that will never happen. She thinks I will change back to my oldself again and she doesn't want to get hurt again. I know I was always kind of lazy and didn't do much, except work on my Fiero. We never really went anywhere, but I didn't think much of it... she never really asked to go places.<P>That Fiero... it had a big part in us meeting and a part in her leaving. When we met I was in my cousin's truck. We talked a little while and it came out that I have a Fiero. Turns out she loves Fieros. I asked her if she wanted to ride in it and she did. They followed us back to our house and I open the garage door to show her my Fiero. The wheels were off of it, but I quickly put it back together, trying to not get dirty. Now my new Fiero is a reason she left. She said we don't need that car. I don't remember exactly what happened, but I didn't buy the car the first day I looked at it. I came home and talked to her and if she would ahve told me to not buy it, I'm sure I wouldn't have.<P>I can't stop thinking about her and all the stupid things I did wrong. She has a huge list of small things I have done over the years that she says I did wrong when I ask "why?" Like she is keeping score.<P>Sorry to ramble on, but I think it helps.<P>Thanks for giving me a shoulder to cry on.||||reg||9
Z||000021||Loki||05-16-2001||09:42 PM||||Rambel on man...I know it helps me to have someone to atleast talk to and have listen....As someone said erlier..Time heals all wounds..<BR><P>------------------<BR>Loki<BR>ICQ# 16477998<BR>www.cox-internet.com/fiero  !!!UPDATED!!!||||reg||1
Z||000022||JSocha||05-16-2001||09:49 PM||||IMO, by doing this "come and go" routine like she is, she is handing you the shovel and wants you to dig yourself deeper into the big pit of depression and pull your strings at the same time while doing it so she knows how to play you later if needed.<P>Hand her the "shovel", close the revolving door, and let her have a "reality" check that either she stays and/or she goes and to "STOP HURTING YOU".<P>I'm sure "Ann Landers" would say the same thing if she was here.<P>Don't mean or want to sound harsh, I just feel "excuses" are being made by you to justify all her actions for what she is now doing to you.<P>Since I have been in a similar boat (GF/BF relationships where they were serious) and where the GF always ended it and went for someone else and "gave me the proverbial hope", I did the same thing you are doing by justifying her every action, fending and defending for her honor (the "you just don't know her... or you just don't understand her...like I do") subsequently digging my pit deeper and making my depression worse and that is what I later learned and came to realize as the big "D" word, aka: "DENIAL".<P>Shoulder is always here for you should you need it.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000023||LZeitgeist||05-17-2001||09:07 AM||||Say it with me...<P>"That which does not kill me makes me stronger."<P>Or at least that's what I keep telling myself. :/<P>My tale of woe is posted in the "Feeling old - am I alone" thread somewhere around here... don't know if it will help you feel better, but you're not alone. We're all in this together.||||reg||1
Z||000024||thomas_l||05-17-2001||09:22 AM||||She's digging up rationalizations to support her decision to leave. Yeah, maybe the Fiero had a part in it so what. There's lots of other stuff too don't forget. Don't fret the small stuff - the notes, the short visit - Loki is right. That is bad juju for you. You need to start thinking like a single man and give her the signal that you are not always going to be available and that is HER choice to leave. Period. Don't get sucked into discussing it or marathon phone calls or 3rd party stuff (like talking with her parents, etc). She's made it real clear she wants a "no risk trial" with the old boyfriend. No f***ing way man! No such thing as a trial run. Time to make you happy and shut the door - at least a little while. If she comes back after a while (months not days) make it on your terms and be very careful. Maybe you both had a part in things leading up to the split but it's her that is focused on some new penis. Let her go and take care of business - you owe it to yourself to not get played.<P>Sorry if that sounds rough, but I've been there and after the first couple of weeks, even my co-workers were saying that I was a completely different person once the stress caused by my ex constantly bothering me eased. And that only happened after I put my foot down and stopped being a convenient stop whenever she felt lonely or horny or both. Find a good porn site and get used to being single  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000025||mbramble||05-17-2001||09:26 AM||||I had page after page of stuff typed but decided to delete it.  It all boiled down to this:<P>She wants a divorce and you won't convince her otherwise.  She wants it to stay friendly because it's easier that way and she probably really doesn't want to hurt you anymore than she already has.  Yet, she doesn't want to be too friendly because you start thinking maybe you are changing her mind and get off track of the subject she really wants to discuss - divorce.  So, she gets cold and distant to convince you that she's serious.  It's a roller coaster that is ridden by lots of folks everyday all over the place and it ends up nowhere --  get off it!!<P>Go see a lawyer and get legal advice on how to proceed and protect yourself once she actually files for divorce.  I would not even discuss divorce with her until you have talked with a lawyer.  She's the one with a new "interest", she wants the divorce, she has moved out of your home -- you should be in good shape.  Well, not really good shape but...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||JohnnyK||05-17-2001||10:14 AM||||I know what your going through (Well, not exactly, since I've never been married) but I can tell.  As harsh as it sounds, **** her.  It's not worth it. Forget the stupid head games she is playing. I can't seem to get myself to do this, but I KNOW it's what should be done. It actually gets easier. (slowly mind you). I agree, there is no such thing as a "trial run".  My famous words.. "Suck it up champ".  My friends started using my own words against me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||LarryB||05-17-2001||10:41 AM||||Since she wants to go, let her.<P>The best way to protect yourself:<P>1) Call a lawyer TODAY and make whatever arrangements are necessary to protect yourself. <P>10 minutes after she leaves for work tomorrow:<P>1) RUN to the nearest hardware store and get new locks for the house. Replace ALL of them.<BR>If you don't know how to do it, get a friend to help you do it or get a locksmith to come over and do it immediately.<P>2) Go to the bank(s), close out all of the accounts.  Go to a different bank and open a new account in YOUR name. <P>3) If you have any joint credit cards, call and CANCEL them. <P>4) Take ANYTHING that could be construed as her personal property, wrap it up, and put it outside or in your garage.  This would include her clothes (plastic bags would be OK to stuff them in), her jewelery, makeup, CDs, etc.  If the computer is hers (or if she's the one that uses it most) put that in the pile.   Document everything, take pictures, write down serial #s of anything reasonably large.  If you have 2 cars, or if you have 1 car and her name is on it, let her take one of them.<P>5) When she comes home he'll realize something is wrong and probably call the cops.  Do NOT let her into the house under ANY circumstances. Its better if you have a good friend or trusted family member there as well, as a witness.<P>When the cops come:<P>a) do NOT let them into the house.<P>b) do NOT come outside of the house.<P>c) get their names, or at least their badge numbers.  If you ask them politely they will at least let you get their numbers off their badges.<P>d) make SURE you let them know you have consulted with an attorney. <P>e) make sure they know that you have taken all her personal belongings and placed them safely in the garage where she can retrieve them.<P>f) if she wants a particular item of her personal property, tell the officers you will get it, close the door, retrive it, and give it to the police.<P>g) if they insist that she be given access to the house, tell them you will do that ONLY if they post a 24 hour guard to ensure that YOUR property is being protected.  They will naturally refuse (we can't do that) so of course you're very sorry but you can't let her in.<P>h) Keep your cell phone handy with the number of your lawyer.  If they get pushy, you may need to call the lawyer, speak to him, then hand the phone to the cops so he can tell them to buzz off. (happened to me a couple of hears ago, the cops took off when the attorney made it clear to them they were going to be explaining their actions to a judge Monday morning).  If at any point the police threaten or warn you with arrest if you continue, ask them to wait a minute because you are going to call your lawyer for advice, then do it IMMEDIATELY using your cell phone. <P>i)If she whines about not knowing where she will spend the night in front of the cops, offer to hand over enough cash for her to rent a hotel room for tomorrow night. <P>You can be pleasant but firm. Don't yell, don't threaten, and most importantly don't get physical.  If she has a car at her disposal, her personal belongings, and a place to spend the night that pretty much takes are of the things they have any kind of authority to intervene in. (You don't have kids which simplifies things a LOT.) They may (in fact probably) will try to intimidate you, but they're looking for easiest way to address the immediate situation. That doesn't take them off the hook though to ensure they protect YOUR property as well, and you may need to remind your lawyer of that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>We've been down this road a couple of times with family members so I'm not just blowing smoke. <P>I know this is harsh, but you've got to look out for #1. It should be obvious by now that she's going to look out for herself, not you.<BR>If you don't take any initiative you'll get screwed. Divorce is a nasty thing and one of the most stressful things you'll ever go through. ||||reg||9
Z||000028||JSocha||05-17-2001||12:23 PM||||Flip side to LarryB:<P>Wife divorced her husband back in 95 (shortly thereafter we met and eventually got hitched) and her brother ended up getting divorced shortly after we got married.<P>Broken_hearted, take LarryB's advise as well as the others here.   My wife's brother got shafted at every corner, she came back "the sorry routine, never do it again" only to leave for yet another guy.  <P>My wife on the otherhad, just to get the Divorce finalized and get her ex out of her life completely gave up everything so she wouldn't be harrassed.  Don't let this happen in reverse.<P>Nuff said.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000029||kong||05-17-2001||04:16 PM|||| I've been there. My first wife through the worst time of my life. I had broken my ankle at work a Tuesday, found out my wife was sleeping with my dad, my uncle and 3 of my friends on weds. Then found out that she had been partying and buying coke with the bill money.<BR> We were buying a house on a personal contract, and she hadn't paid a house payment in 4 months. When they kicked me out of the house I didn't have anywhere to park my cars, so the city towed them off at the owners request.<BR> So I lost my wife, my house, 2 of my cars, irrepairable damage to my family, a broken ankle, and wound up losing my job over all that happened. All within 2 weeks. I was so tore up that I had considered saying the hell with it.<BR> But , I held on. I'm glad I did, I now have the wife that I'll be with till the the end. She has stood by me through thick and thin for 23 years, and there have been some pretty thin times for us. We have 2 great kids, and the most beautiful grand daughter you ever laid eyes on. What I'm trying to say with this sad tale is, After all this is resolved, the only way to go is up. <BR> They say that time heals all wounds. That's not true. It still hurts to think back on those times. But I look at what I have now, It makes me glad to be alive. You can't let this ruin your life. It WILL get better. Trust me. The best days of your life are AHEAD of you, not behind. So try to grit your teeth and get through this and you'll see the truth in it<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||JSocha||05-17-2001||05:21 PM||||Broken_Hearted,<P>Please leave a post so we all know you are at least still with us...<P>You haven't responded in a day and at least I, if not the others as well, want to know if you are okay!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||Broken_hearted||05-18-2001||01:26 AM||||I'm still here and not gonna do anything stupid.(I hope)<P>Today she was here when I got home. Remember the note she left about wanting to talk? Well, all day I was hoping it was to try to work something out... nope! she wanted to talk about the divorce. I started trying to convince her that this isn't right and I have changed. She is worried that I will "change" back into my old self still. We talked and cried and then went out to eat. I know she will never change her mind, but I keep trying. This only makes her angry. And then she makes me angry. I can't believe she doesn't want to work this out... 9 years down the toilet.<P>There is an up-side to all this(I hope). I called my highschool sweetheart the other day. We talked a while about the past (we dated for over a year when we were 17 or so) and she seemed happy to hear from me. Lucky me, she hasn't ever been married and no kids. She was very smart and now has a good job and when I asked her why she wasn't married she said she hasn't found anyone worth dating in 2 years, and she said she thinks her standards might be too high. This has me worried that that might rule me out. My wife has my moral very low, and rejection just plain ole sucks! I got her e-mail address and wrote her a letter about a week later. Tell me what you think this means, this is her words when I told her that I might go there to see my brother soon. She wrote:<P>...I was glad to hear from you when you called.  If you feel like calling<BR>again, please do (if we can think of anything to say to each other).  And if<BR>you come to see your brother, and if you have time while you are here, I<BR>would like to see you again, <B>just to see you</B>.  So let me know when you are<BR>coming, or if you do.  And keep in touch with me if you can.<P>See the bold print? What do you think that means? When we dated we were always together or on the phone. She ended up breaking up with me and I took it very hard. About a week later she called me and we got back together, but eventually I broke it off and ended up moving away. After about 10 years I call her out of the blue. I am so nervous and not even sure what I want from her, but I know I still have feelings for her... or do I? I don't really even know what she looks like anymore, and her for me too. When we were dating she was very pretty and I'm sure she still is. We were talking and she commented everything goes to her hips... I wonder if she has gained weight? Not that it would matter. I remember her alot lately, but I know I am not ready to get anything started with her yet, and I really don't want to hurt her or get hurt. I think I am just looking for somebody to talk to and maybe take it from there. If I could be happy with someone, it could be her. I want to talk to her very bad, but I am going to take it slow and just email her once in a while for now.<P>-Taking it one breathe at a time<BR>(I wouldn't wish what I am going through on my worst enemy!)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||JSocha||05-18-2001||01:53 AM||||She apparently wants to see you, perhaps even to test the waters with you to see where it goes and how you may have matured over time.<P>Don't talk about what you are going through now.  Will be a big turn off to her.  Focus on what she has been doing all these years.  If she asks, keep it short, don't elaborate or go into details.  Make it like you don't want to talk about that subject as eventually you'll dump the load on to her porch where she was only trying to be conversational.<P>Remember also, you are on the "rebound" which isn't good either, because as individuals in this situations, we tend to latch to another relationship and try to recreate it so we feel comfortable.  Therefore your interest as well should be to keep it as a Platonic friendship.  If it doesn't work out later on and over time, who knows, she may just introduce you to a very good friend who she feels you may get along with.  Or the other possibility, keep her at bay and give her the challenge.  If she wants to proceed further with the friendship, just let her know you want to make sure you are over your past enough so as not to interfere with a relationship with her.<P>Now if I recall from your letter, the last time you broke up, it was on your grounds for the break up, therefore, who knows, she may be thinking about you all these years. <P>Keep that in mind, yet do not pursue it verbally.  She will hint towards that if that is on her mind.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good to hear from you.  And "WE HOPE" you don't do anything stupid as well.  <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000033||LarryB||05-18-2001||04:33 AM||||I'd pretty much agree with JSocha, except perhaps I see her message as being a clear warning that although she would enjoy seeing you, she's telling you in no uncertain terms that she's just not interested if you're looking to try to get something going.  She's almost certainly suspicious at this point and her radar is going to be aimed directly at you as well.<P>Not that she can't change her mind, (remember the rule that the woman is allowed to change her mind at any time for any reason  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ) but at least for now having ideas about maybe getting back together with her is a bad move.<P>Better to prioritize on the immediate problem of getting separated from your wife before really looking for someone else as well. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000034||JSocha||05-18-2001||09:23 AM||||Not to read into the letter he received or give BH false hope, but here is how I broke it apart.  FieroLisa and/or Dynayee (sp?) may have to get involved in this one to give a ladies perspective, cause only a woman knows whats on a womans mind.<P>...I was glad to hear from you when you called. If you feel like calling<BR>again, please do (if we can think of anything to say to each other). <P><I>IMO, she defintately enjoyed hearing from him and would like him to call, even if they do not have much to say.</I><P>And if you come to see your brother, and if you have time while you are here, I<BR>would like to see you again, just to see you. <P><I>Assuming BH put the BOLD on the letter here and/or it did or did not come from her, yet it is a clear indication she is interested in talking with BH and "she would definately like to see him."  Again, perhaps to test the waters or maybe because their frienship was very good when they were younger that she is concerened about him.</I><P>So let me know when you are coming, or if you do. <P><I>She wants to know when BH will be there or is there.</I><P>And keep in touch with me if you can.<P><I>IMO, if BH is not to busy she would like to hear from him at any chance.</I><P>Realize, being the Co-dependent that I am, perhaps my co-dependent side is reading more into this letter then any of you are or even BH.  <P>Therefore, be part of this codependent thread ladies and help this man out with the translation of this letter (from a women's point of view), cause I myself have still not figured women out after 33 years.  I may have gotten this all wrong as well!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000035||thomas_l||05-18-2001||09:27 AM||||I'd second LarryB - get things resolved with the your (soon to be ex?) wife before you go looking at other women. It will make it easier for you to focus on taking care of business and not add more stress to an already difficult situation. I guarantee if you get some closure to the matter at hand, it will sure make pursuing new women alot easier. Take time for yourself to do some shopping and make sure you don't repeat the past.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Butter||05-18-2001||12:50 PM||||If you love something set it free.<BR>If it comes back to you, it yours.<BR>If it does not, it never was.<BR>Act respectible while your waiting to see what she really wants to do. This is so you will have the clout to make her feel good about returning or bad for leaving.<BR>Oh yea, Be Tough cause divorce $uck$ Emotion, Money, Time, and it hurts.  ||||reg||9
Z||000037||Broken_hearted||05-18-2001||04:03 PM||||I appreciate all your concern and advise, but I guess I don't listen very well. Last night when my wife and I were out, all I could do it try to reason with her to stay. That only makes her want to leave even more(she HAS made up her mind and WILL leave no matter what I say or do) and this makes me fight harder and makes her push away even more. When she moved out she said it was to "think" about what she wants to do. She always said she was confused and wasn't sure what she wanted. This really tore me up because when I asked her if she wanted to remain my wife she would say, "I don't know." When I asked her if she wanted me to give up she said, "No." After she moved out she was calling this guy and I know he is the reason for all this and for her to leave all we have and have been though just doesn't sit well. I guess I can't accept it. I have no freaking clue what I am going to do without her, especially knowing that this could have been worked out.<P>As far as the old girlfriend(Susan) goes: When I called her we laughed about all our old times together and I tried to find a clue as to what she might think about me after all this time. I told her I am divorced already... I paniced. Lying to her already isn't a good sign, but I just wanted to talk to her and I didn't want to let her know I am going through it right now. Maybe that will come back to haunt me, but I will tell her the truth and the reason I lied about it as soon as I get to know her a little better again. <P>In that letter, I made those words bold to let you know the part that has me confused. And when I called her, I told her I was thinking about coming to see my brother and she said, "You should come see him." I kind of took that as a hint that she might want to see me and she thinks I should come see her as well. In her letter she more or less asked me to call her again without me asking if I could... that has me excited. I don't plan to rush into this if anything does happen, but I also don't want to miss an opportunity like this, and I sure don't want to be alone.<P>My wife and I get along so good and I love her with all my heart and soul... I tell her this, but she kind of frowns and tells me she is sorry. We had a lack of communication - that's for sure, and I guess I took her for granted. I figured we would be together always and forever and we would get out of this rut we were in, but it was no rut, it was doom. All I can do is think of her and all she can do is think of getting away from me. I feel like I have nothing to keep me going. <P>I used to work on my Fiero and loved doing it, now I can't even drive it. After I got the car to where I knew it was reliable (I put the egine in it) I let her drive it because she loved driving that car, but she thinks I didn't want her driving it. I actually bought and built this car for us, but she thinks I wanted it for myself. She thinks all I have ever done was to make myself happy and her sad/mad. I just can't figure this one out. All I know is life sucks right about now and the pain is almost too unbearable.<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000038||thomas_l||05-18-2001||04:50 PM||||Don't try to analyze the old girlfriends meaning. You need to shelve that for a while and deal with the here and now. As far as the Fiero and who it's for - fuggetaboudit. That car is just stuff. Either think of it as yours or sell it and get another one that is definitely yours. If it's hers, sign the title and mail it to her, park it in the street and lock your spare keys in it. It's just an object. When I split with my ex-wife we had a '86 Escort GT (my car) and a '77 280Z (her car). She had two daughters (previous marriage) and I swapped cars with her and didn't feel bad about it. It was real simple: 3 people don't fit in a 2 seater. The Escort was in better shape so she made out better anyway. The Z car looked cool but it was really a piece of crap. I drove it a year fixing up a few things and ended up horsetrading for a '77 Mercedes 450SEL.<P>Your assignment is: re-read everyones advice here until you can recite it. Then do it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> In the mean time, spend this weekend with your parents or other family or at the very least go visit someone you know and trust on the Fiero forum and get away a day or two.<P>Take care!||||reg||1
Z||000039||rogergarrison||05-18-2001||05:20 PM||||Its great. I got divorced 20 some years ago, and its the one best thing I ever did. I got more money, more time, more toys than ever. Lots more fun just hanging out with whoever. Id never even consider getting married again...well maybe a supermodel with a few million in the bank. Otherwise im staying single and happy doing whatever and whenever I want. Move on as fast as you can and itll be great. (side bar is itll piss her off to know your so happy without her, revenge is sweet)||||reg||1
Z||000040||DRH||05-18-2001||05:25 PM||||The old girlfriend should definitely go on the back burner for a while. You need to get your head on straight first. <P>Don't read too much into what your wife says is/was wrong right now. She is probably very confused herself and is just throwing stuff out there because you keep pressing her. If you see her again... BACK OFF, keep it light and friendly, DON'T analyze, DON'T ask questions, DON'T beg, DON'T plead, etc... If you haven't already figured it out, the harder you try the worse it will get. <P>You CAN be without a wife/girlfriend. Your not knowing how to live without her is probably part of what's driving her away. That's a lot of pressure on someone. You need to learn how to be the guy that would really like to be with her but can also make it just fine without her. It may be too late for this one, but you will eventually meet someone else and you don't want history repeating...<P>Spend time with friends and family... Make new friends (NO serious relationship though!!!)... Go to a Fiero show... Go fishing... Do volunteer work...<P>You CAN do it. Good luck!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000041||Broken_hearted||05-19-2001||02:42 PM||||The old girlfriend thing is nothing more than something to keep my head up and something to look forward to. I guess it gives me hope. Don't worry, I don't plan on jumping into something anytime soon, but at the same time, I can't help to think it might be some kind of sign.<P>Yesterday my wife stopped by and I tried talking to her, but ended up trying to reason with her again. She got all upset and I soon got my head on straight and stopped the madness. I then asked her if she wanted to go do something, she wanted to go eat and see a movie. I commented that I would like to go out and eat because that's the only time I ever get a descent meal (joking - she isn't the best cook). She smiled and my heart sank as she started to cheer up. I love to see her smile instead of tears. She wanted to have Chinese food and I agreed, she can ask for anything right now and I would do anything to get/do it for her. It was great, like old times again. As I waited to pay the check she stood next to me and I hugged her. She rememberd her bother's baby was coming home and couldn't go see the movie, but we stopped by the store to get some video tapes. Anytime we stopped I hugged her and somtimes whispered in her ear how much she means to me and how I can't believe this is happening. Everytime I hugged her, she hugged back - I'm gonna miss that. I know this is wrong and I think she thinks it is too, this shouldn't end, not like this. It just feels completely wrong.<P>I am thinking about calling her to come see that movie tonight. Hopefully I can spend as much time with her as possible without me trying to convince her to not leave me, and hopefully she might see the light, but not likely. She is determined that life without me is going to be much better - maybe it will.<P>God give me strength...<P><p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 05-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000042||AkursedX||05-19-2001||03:56 PM||||I am truly sorry to hear about this story.  The only positive thing I could say is that, at least you still have some hope and that you can still talk to her.  <BR>I had a girlfriend of a year and a half, and we broke up and became the best of friends.  I have never had someone like her in my life.  She was the most important thing to me, and we both knew one day that even though we weren't dating, that we would marry each other eventually.  But, that will never happen now.  She killed herself about a year ago.  <BR>So in conclusion, protect yourself like everyone else has said, and just try to take things one day at a time.  Who knows what will happen, but try to understand that fighting isn't going to bring her back.  Only fate will.  And if it isn't your fate to have her back, then move on to something else.  I am more than sure you will be able to make it through this.  It might seem like the end of the world now, but time will heal the hurt, and you will be the wiser for the experience.  Live and Learn||||reg||1
Z||000043||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||08:00 PM||||This is a sad story for sure. Here's what I think is the saddest part:<BR>From what you've said, she misses the romance. Romance is great, but it's fleeting. We can't possibly live a romance filled life forever, it's not practical. That's not to say we can't be romantic at times, but it just doesn't work as a lifestyle. The upside is, that we often exchange the passion and fire of young romance for the safety and security of a loving relationship, and all the things it can offer. The sad part is, she apparently doesn't realize this, or, that's not the kind of relationship she wants, preferring the former to the latter. The other thing is, we often try to love people the way we want to be loved. I run into this with my current g/f alot. I'm kind of an action guy. I like to buy her cards, and do things for her, and show her how I feel through action. While she appreciates those things, they aren't what make her feel loved. What makes her feel the most loved is two things: for me to tell her that I love her, and to let her do things for me. And it's hard, because I feel almost guilty in letting her do for me all the time. But that's what makes her happy. Obviously, there's a great deal more to it than this, but you get the idea.<BR>Sadly, you're only choice is to let her go. And as hard as it is, I can tell you from experience, your constant exposure to her, and the exchanges of affection aren't helping you. I know what a relief it is to hold someone you love, especially in pain. She probably feels the same thing. As hard as it is, my experience is that to effectively recover from a broken relationship, I had to do a couple of things.<BR>First was to accept it was over. Period. As long as I held on to any notion that we would reconcile, I could never recover, or move on. (this isn't saying that we may never reconcile, obviously, anything is possible. But as far as I'm concerned, we will never be together again, and today, I'm OK with that)<BR>Once I accepted it was over, I cut off contact. Before that, she and I would do what you talk about. We would get together once in a while to "talk" and discuss our situation and (inevitibly) our pain. We would go out to eat, or do things. That wasn't helpful, it was more hurtful than anything else. It felt good for the moment, but the future demise was always eminent. When I realized that, I made the choice to cut off all communication. It wasn't easy, but it was necessary, and went a long way towards helping me to actually recover from the loss, and move on with my life. When she would call, I wouldn't answer the phone. I let her leave a message, and if it was in my opinion necessary for me to talk to her, I would try to call when I knew she wasn't there so I could just leave her a message. This may sound trivial or juvinile, but I didn't (don't) see it that way, it was important to my recovery.<BR>As is evident by the heartfelt responses you've recieved in this thread, obviously you have the support of many people here who feel your pain, including me. I've been there. (who hasn't) <P>Namaste<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000044||Broken_hearted||05-20-2001||10:02 PM||||I think she was trying to make this as easy on me as possible (she told me that) by taking to seperation process slow. That only gave me something to cling to, and gave me hope. When she finally made up her mind to divorce me and move on, I couldn't accept that. She gave me hope and a reason to fight, but that's gone now. In the beginning I didn't think we would end up divorced, maybe her friends have something to do with that. I try to let her go, but I don't want that.<P>I want to change the locks on the doors and tell her to only contact me through an attorney, but I just can't bring myself to it. She is here everyday, but leaves before I get home, and that really bugs me for some reason. <P>I told her to come back to me if it doesn't work out between them. She said, "Why would you want me back after what I did to you?" I uttered, "You just don't get it, do you? I love you more than anything!" Now she says she wouldn't come back to me if it doesn't work out.<P>Like I said - this shouldn't be happening. All my freinds that know us agree, they're floored when I tell them what is happening.<P>I have made alot of mistakes, said things I shouldn't have, and have too many regrets... too many things to make up for. I don't want to make another one by giving up, but I guess I have no choice. How can I go on without her? My love, my best friend, my everything is gone.||||reg||1
Z||000045||Taijiguy||05-20-2001||10:23 PM||||If I might be so bold as to assume to give you some advice- don't blame yourself!<BR>I see in your words that you're trying to be responsible for her actions. She made this choice, not you. You can't regret things you've done, everyone makes mistakes, and we all say and do things we wish we hadn't. But you can't beat yourself up or them. She made the choice to leave. She gave up, not you. Whatever her reasons, good, bad or indifferent, they are still her choices. It sucks that you have to suffer with her in her choices, but that's the risk of being in love. We take that chance every time we hand our heart to someone. <BR>I don't know the reason for her coming there every day, but it would bother me too. If she is getting things from the house, she should get them all at once and be done with it. <BR>Again, presuming to be able to give advice, I can tell you why it made me feel better when I gave up on being with my ex. Empowerment. I realized that by holding on to my relationship with her, I was giving her all my power. She was in control of my destiny. When I finally made the decision it was over for myself, I felt strong, and in control again. I think if you take control of the things you can take control of, you'll feel much better. Obviously I wouldn't suggest you do anything that will cause you problems, nor am I suggesting you become mean or vengeful or difficult. Just to take control of your life, and make your own choices, and not leave everything up to her. <BR>My dad used to have an expression I hated. He used to tell me to "suck it up". As much as I hated it when he said that to me, eventually, it would sink in, and I would realize he was right. <BR>Pop the sunroof (or tee's) out of your Fiero, point it towards the nearest highway, and just cruise for a couple of hours. Nothing I've ever done could clear my head like that. No radio. Just the sound of the engine and exhaust. Those are things that make sense. Every sound has a meaning, and a purpose, not like life, which sometimes just doesn't make sense.<BR>You'll pull trough this. <BR>~Taiji<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000046||Slick781||05-20-2001||11:45 PM||||Well, I know I'm just a teenager and don't have any great relationship advice... but i do know the one person you need to talk to before you ever talk to her, your friends, or a lawyer... and that's God.<P>I really feel that you just need to go kneel down at your bed, and just start praying... don't pray that she will come back to you, or anything selfish like that.  Just pray to God that he will help you do whatever HE wants you to do.  If you haven't already, give your life to him, if you have, make sure you tell him AND yourself that your life is his, and whatever he decides to do with it is the right thing.<P>If you don't mind, I'd like to reccomend that you go to <A HREF="http://www.calvarychapel.org" TARGET=_blank>www.calvarychapel.org</A>  and see if there is one in your area.  It's a nondenominational church, so they just spit out the truth, and let you interpret it.  I have been going to mine for about 6 months now, and it has really changed my life.<P>If you just do as He asks, He will work wonders for you, I promise... and i NEVER promise anything to anyone.<P>God Bless you, bud.||||reg||1
Z||000047||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||03:03 PM||||About 10 years ago my wife and I were going through some rough times, and I was reading a book on how to make your marrage better. There was a chapter on things guys do that upset and alienate their wives. The author said all guys do some of these things, and the problem is, when you offend your wife in some small way, and dont even realize it, and never ask forgiveness, and never correct the problem, its like your wife takes one small step away from you. <P>  after alot of offenses, and a lot of time, a lot of small steps really add up and there is a huge space between you. He said if a marrage last through the first year, and these small things are happening, then it all blows up around the seventh year.<P>  the author had a list of 125 things guys do that upset or offend theirs wives, usually without even realizing it. I said, okay, lets see how many i have done.<P>  started reading the list, oh, ive done that, that too, yep done that......OakWrap, done that a lot....<P>  it think I hit about 100 out of the 125 things. Mind you, I think of myself as a great guy, and I thought I was a great husband. Not that Im a slimball or anything - I just never realized how differently men and women look at things, and how things that are no big deal to guys are extreemly important to women.<P>  We are in some ways victims of the sexaul revolution of the 60s, and the womans lib women-and-men-are-the-same BS from the 70s and 80s.<P>  Bottom line is, it is really hard to develop and maintain a postive and meaningful relationship between a man and a women, esp if you had no role models to learn from as a kid growing up (my parents were divorced, my wifes parents were disfunctional). Trying to figure out what is important and how to do everything right as you are doing it is impossible.<P> Or as someone once said, experience is a cruel teacher: first you are tested - then you get the lessons.<P>  From you posts you mentioned you lived with your wife for a few years and planned to get married sometime. This is normal for our times - my wife an I did the same thing - and you have no idea how much damage this does to a marriage! A woman needs to feel that she is THE most important thing in the WORLD to her husband. Having a romantic courtship and getting married before you have sex and/or live together (i know how corny this sounds) tells your wife that you love her - only her - there will never be anyone else for you but her.<P>  But when you have sex before marriage, and you live together, what is that saying to her? Maybe you are the one, maybe not, we'll try this out for a while, if it doesnt work out I can always dump you and find someone else....<P>  statistics have shown that couples that lived together before being married are far more LIKELY to get divorced!<P>  Okay, I dont want to make you feel like I am putting your marriage problems all on your shoulders. your wife went along with the arrangement too, I would suspect she has some self esteem issues, otherwise when you proposed that the two of you move in together, she would have told you to take a flying leap!<P>  You are down and you are depressed and you want this to be all someone elses fault, but you also want to understand what went wrong - and if you did fail in some of your responsibilities you want to learn from your mistakes so your next relationship (with whoever) will be better than this one turned out.<P>   Im am certain you think you are a great guy, and your friends will all tell you this is all your wifes fault, or the other guys fault. I am also certain your wife has a list of greivances against you.<P>  You cannot solve this problem on your own. If you could you would not have fallen into it in the first place. You have been telling your wife you can change, that you have changed.<P>  If you want to take a shot at saving your marriage you are gonna have to make it clear to your wife that she is the most important thing in the world to you, and that you want to make HER happy, and that you are willing to do whatever it takes to make HER happy (not to make yourself stop hurting).<P>  this means you will have a lot to learn. If you are serious about saving your marriage go see a marriage counceller, not a lawyer. See one on your own first, and try to get a handle on what has gone wrong with your marrage. Remember this is not someone to *****  and complain to about what a great husband you were and now why is she doing this to you. Go and find out what you both did wrong - go with a spirit of 'we both screwed up and I want to learn why - ego be dammed"<P>  then if the counceller thinks there is hope, tell your wife (where ever she is) that you have been seeing a professional marriage counceller, and, if she is willing, you would like her to come with you ONCE to talk with him and see if he thinks you marrage can be saved. Make it clear this is not a commitment on her part - that you are gonna go together and talk it out with a professional, and then decide together where you want to go from there.<P>  You cant figure out how to have a great marriage on your own anymore than you could figure out 4 dimensional calculus on your own. You take your tranny to a pro when it needs help - you go to a doctor for serious health problems - you need a pro for this too.<P>  The real assesment you need to make right now is whether your marriage is too far gone (too rusted out) to be worth restoring. Dont jump to the obvious conclusion (you will instinctive want to say yes cause you are hurting right now). Your marriage is flat line right now, dead, and the paper work is just a formality. If you and your wife want to start over and rebuild it, you will be starting from less than zero, cause you are gonna have to undo all that damage between you two first, all the nasty yucky stuff has to be cleared out first, then you can start to build a new marriage.<P>  dont take that lightly. It will most likely be easier for both of you to go your separate ways, and to start working on new relationships.<P>  but whether you stay together, or split up, get some help brother! Find a marriage counceler or personal counceler you can talk to and spend some serious time and effort (and $$$) and figure out what went wrong, and what you could have done instead, and your next relationship will be 1000% better, whether your next relationship is with your wife, or someone new.||||reg||1
Z||000048||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||03:22 PM||||One last bit of advice - dont sit and stew over the fact that this is not how its suppose to be - that it isnt fair.<P>you marriage is like a totalled car right now. You could spend years going over each part of it and crying over each part, "this part shouldnt be like this, this isnt right, why is it like this..."<P>things are the way they are - you cant go back and change them - you gotta take life from where it is today and go forward with it - take what you have now and make the best of it.<P>  dont stew over how it was suppose to be, or that it shouldnt of happened this way. Youll just make yourself crazy doing that.<P> Pick up the pieces and make the best out of what you have. cause in reality, life isnt 'suppose' to be like anything - life is what it is. <P>Whats important is how you deal with it, and how you respond to wherever life takes you.<P>when really rough times fall on you, that is when you reveal your true nature.||||reg||1
Z||000049||Broken_hearted||05-22-2001||08:42 PM||||I wish I would have heard that advice 9 years ago... everything you said puts everything together now, but it's too late.<P>She refuses to see anyone about this - it's over!<P>We could have rebuilt this marriage because I see where I went wrong - the little things (going to the grocery store with her... etc.) added up, and she kept score. I have learned a lesson (I took notes!) and I will apply these to my next relationship, but I still don't want anyone but her.<P>You (Ken Wittlief) have put down the most important words ever to be admitted to this Forum, and I hope that anyone that is married will read it and LISTEN!<P>-Broken_hearted (forever)||||reg||1
Z||000050||Ken Wittlief||05-22-2001||11:38 PM||||If its over for cetain them I am sorry for the pain you are going through. You are correct - no one will ever replace your wife, just as no one can ever replace a lost child or close friend. <P>  But you will have other relationships, you will have friends and coworkers and neighbors and someday you will have another romatic relationship - it wont be the same - how could it be?<P>  there are what, 4 or 5 billion people in the world? sometimes we feel there is only one 'right' person for us out there.<P>  the truth is, if you get your life together, get your career and interests in focus, decide what things are important to you and pick out a destination for your life, you can start heading for it makeing whatever progress you can, along the way you will meet hundreds, thousands even, of people that will be friends, kindred spirits, and potential romantic interests. <P>  Its kinda funny, before I met my wife its like this search program was running in the back of my mind, looking for a possible lover/mate/spouse - always on watch, always checking every glance and expression on womens faces to detect a possible connection.<P>  then when I proposed to my wife, I noticed the next day it was as if that program had halted - it was very noticable, as if 85% of my mental processing power had been tied up with that single task, and now all that energy was free for other things.<P>   I have been married for 20 years now, and its funny, but every few years or so i will meet a lady or co-worker and when i get to know her a little bit, its like that program kicks on for a second, and a light blinks as if to say "if you werent married now, this would be a great candidate".<P>  Not that Im looking or anything like that - its just another verification that there is not only one person out there for us, there are many many people that are 'right' for us.<P>  You probabally dont see this now, and dont really want to think about it. You've been through a lot. Even if you know you cannot save your marriage I still recommend you go find a personal counceller you can talk to and sort out what went wrong.<P>   there is something strange about human nature. We are in charge of our own lives almost completely, so we have to depend on our own judgment. If we felt we were going around making bad decisions all the time, making constant mistakes, then we wouldnt be able to function. We wouldnt be able to trust our own judgment.<P>   But we do make mistakes - if we deny that we condemm ourselves to repeat them. so there has to be points in our lives where we stop and say, before today I though X was the right thing to do, but now after doing X I have learned that Y would have been the right thing to do.<P>  call it growing or changing or maturing, but it is not an easy thing to do - its like saying "yesterday I was crazy but Im ok now". Our egos dont like looking at our past mistakes, errors or failures.<P>  a counceler can help you with this, help you sort it all out. Its not an easy thing to do at first. Guys are suppose to be tough and independant and figure everything out on our own - its hard to go to someone and tell them your life just blew up in your face, and your not really sure how it happened.<P>  In the long run its the easiest thing, the best way to go - you are not the first guy in the world to go through this, and you dont have to go through this alone.||||reg||1
Z||000051||Fierochic88||05-24-2001||12:01 AM||||I've been too busy reading some of this extremely good advice to post...but now that I'm finally caught up I will.  Don't give up.  While things may be looking bad in life right now, they WILL get better...maybe not tonight..or tomorrow..or next week/month whatver...but sometime they will get better.  If things are over from her end, there is nothing you can do to change that...while I have yet to actually get married...I have been in a serious enough relationship to know that no matter how hard you hope and pray you can't change another person's thoughts...especially someone who seems to have thought this out for a while before springing it upon you...and while that is a cruel tactic (one which I found out that hard way) sometimes it is the defense mechanism of someone who realizes that while they care about you there is someone better for you whether or not you realize it.  I think you have already realized one of the most important things.  You mentioned that you "took notes" and will apply them to your next relationship...whenever that may be..be sure to do just that!  Every relationship in life, be it marriage or friends is about learning...some relationships end well while others end poorly...but you can gain valuable experience from each one as long as you sit back and reflect on it when the time is right.||||reg||3
Z||000052||Fierochic88||05-24-2001||12:01 AM||||I've been too busy reading some of this extremely good advice to post...but now that I'm finally caught up I will.  Don't give up.  While things may be looking bad in life right now, they WILL get better...maybe not tonight..or tomorrow..or next week/month whatver...but sometime they will get better.  If things are over from her end, there is nothing you can do to change that...while I have yet to actually get married...I have been in a serious enough relationship to know that no matter how hard you hope and pray you can't change another person's thoughts...especially someone who seems to have thought this out for a while before springing it upon you...and while that is a cruel tactic (one which I found out that hard way) sometimes it is the defense mechanism of someone who realizes that while they care about you there is someone better for you whether or not you realize it.  I think you have already realized one of the most important things.  You mentioned that you "took notes" and will apply them to your next relationship...whenever that may be..be sure to do just that!  Every relationship in life, be it marriage or friends is about learning...some relationships end well while others end poorly...but you can gain valuable experience from each one as long as you sit back and reflect on it when the time is right.<P>Jennifer||||reg||3
Z||000053||WisconsinGT||05-24-2001||03:49 AM||||Damn! What a great thread. I have to say that the people here are fantastic. You all have given this man the greatest gift he could ever receive. <P>Broken_hearted, I can relate to what you are going through. I too, am going through the same thing. Actually, I had a very similar experience. Only, I downloaded a program that recorded every keystroke on my computer to find out what was going on. And yes, it was a mutual friend of ours. I did not have any pain as I was in divorce mode prior to viewing my wife's e-mails. But after viewing them and seeing what they were saying to each other I was extremely upset. I can honestly tell you that time will work in your favor. <P>That stuff happened about a year and six months ago. Since then she has and still does call me asking if we can get back together, or see a marriage consoler. (Keep in mind that she was very much in favor of getting a divorce.) It gets better believe me. <P>I too was no saint. We lived in 5 different countries. I was always dealing with new customs and trying to fit in. I did not give her very much attention. Communication was nil. &lt;------#1 mistake many men and women do.   I took her for granted. &lt;-----#2 mistake. I thought of myself.&lt;-----#3 mistake. I could go on and on. <P>I look back now and have learned alot about myself and the opposite sex. I have fallen in love with a wonderful women. She means the world to me and I let her know it both verbally and through my actions. I found out that what I had before was not true love but a need that I was trying to fulfill at that time in my life. Marriage means something totally different to me now. Why, because I actually thought about it! <P>It is a funny world we live in. I have seen alot of things in my 37 years of life. And I never really know what is around the corner for me. But I now have the power to see myself before I get to that corner. <P>Anyway, be true to yourself, listen to the words that are spoken to you. And follow the advice of the wonderful people in this forum.  Let her go and free your mind. You will get beyond this!<P>By the way, the court date for my divorce is August 13th. That's the end of that chapter. ||||reg||1
Z||000054||Ken Wittlief||05-25-2001||05:13 PM||||Broken Heart: stay in touch - there is a lot more stuff I can tell you about my own experiences, since i am using my real name on here, and address, i dont want to put too much personal stuff on the web.<P> If you wanna talk about things more email me directly. And let the rest of us on the forum know how its going from time to time.<P>-Ken||||reg||1
Z||000055||Broken_hearted||05-27-2001||12:25 PM||||Everyone tells me this will get easier as time goes on. It's not getting better, only harder - each day is a struggle.<P>I'm hanging in there though. ||||reg||1
Z||000056||LarryB||05-27-2001||01:09 PM||||Hang in there, bro'.  If you make it through this you'll be able to handle whatever comes your way.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000057||JSocha||05-27-2001||03:36 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Broken_hearted:<BR><B>Everyone tells me this will get easier as time goes on. It's not getting better, only harder - each day is a struggle.<P>I'm hanging in there though. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You just personally haven't bottomed out emotionally yet to where your subconcious survival instict kicks in to say subconsiously, "Get the heck out of this rutt your in, learn and move on".  Then it's all up hill from there once this happens, so hang on in there!  It will happen and you will know when it does, cause life looks sunnier on the otherside of the hill thereafter.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000058||MooCow||05-27-2001||10:27 PM||||First off I can say I feel for you, but honestly don't know how you must feel.  I just turned 19 and have lots to learn.  I myself have never been in love per say.  This post as actually moved me in a way I can't describe.<BR>I feel after reading all the posts you need to stop blamming yourself.  Yes, you both made mistakes and you can't say that it is all your fault.  It seems she has already made up her mind and there is nothing you are going to be able to do to change that.  All you are doing now by stalling is making her more angry at you.  Things happen for a reason whether it is good or bad, they just happen.  You can't change how you acted in the past.  Only learn from how you acted.  Don't keep saying "If I would have done this", or "Said I loved her more".  All you do then is hurt yourself more.  Like everyone says time will heal your heart.<BR>I am a strong believer in fate.  If your wife wants a divorce then you should let her go.  Be a freind to her not an enemy.  Don't push yourself upon her. If this was meant to be then it will turn out in the end.  <BR>Also your 7 years together was not a waste.  All those memories you will carry with you forever, the good and the bad.  Life is full of memories.  Just get your head straight and things will work out for the best.<BR>Now to me its weird how your Ex girlfriend and you are now chatting after all these years.  I don't know if its fate or just simply dumb luck, time will tell.<BR>Do not jump right into another relationship till this all gets disolved.  Don't use your Ex girlfreind for a rebound girl or to try to prove to your wife that you have moved on as well.<BR>I don't know how good advise this is comming from a 19 year old that has yet to fall in love, but this is what I think.<BR>I truly hope things work out for the best.  Please keep in touch.<P>                               Thanks Ken<p>[This message has been edited by MooCow (edited 05-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000059||Broken_hearted||06-01-2001||12:46 AM||||Today is not a good day.<P> I found out today that my wife has sent an email to her boyfriend telling her that they are booked (plane tickets to Reno)and she can't wait to see him, and she loves him. I called her parents to let them know about this guy (they had no clue) and that she is going to see him before the divorce. I talked to her dad, and he is not happy. He knows of the guy from a long time ago and does not like him at all! He was shocked when I told him about the other guy, he just sat there in silence on the phone. I'm glad I got it off my chest, but I also think I told them to try to get her back, but I think it will make her really hate me. I just wanted them to know the whole story. Come to find out she told them she was going with a girlfriend of hers. I don't know what is going to happen now - I'm sure this is over forever. Something wants me to get even now. I want to make her life a living hell, and at the same time I want her back. <P>What a mess...<P><p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000060||Mach10||06-01-2001||01:57 AM||||Hang in there, buddy. It won't last forever. <P>My advice to you would be to go see some professional help. You are tearing yourself apart. Go see someone to talk to. Don't wait till you are at your lowest. <P>Find something to do, rather than skulking around the house looking for things to make you miserable. Find something that makes you happy.<P>I'd suggest a puppy. Seriously! Find something that needs a lot of attention. A puppy has the advantage of that he/she will love you back no matter what happens. That's what you need at this point. <P>Honestly, she's walking all over you. I'm not saying she's a bad person, but you are playing the victim, and letting her get away with everything. Time to put your foot down. Change the locks. I'd call her an tell her that her stuff's in the garage, personally, but everything else rests. Don't get trampled on. The only way to get her back, if at all, is to <B><I>*END*</I></B> this relationship. Whatever happens later is a new start.<P>Good luck, and rest assured that our thoughs(and prayers, if applicable) go with you. You can win this out.<P>Just remember, that you don't have to do it alone. IF it starts getting too bad, go get some help. <P>Take it easy!||||reg||6
Z||000061||Raydar||06-01-2001||09:07 AM||||Get a lawyer. Now. Cover your ass.<BR>I apologize if this sounds cynical, but she's already proven that she can't be trusted. I wouldn't put anything past her. Time for you to look out for yourself. <BR>Go see a marriage counselor. I'm sure you need someone to talk with. <P>Maybe say a prayer? <BR>That's up to you. Works for me.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000062||LarryB||06-01-2001||10:49 AM||||Raydar's right. Do it NOW. She'll probably go supernova when she finds out you told her parents.<P>Oh, make sure you copy that logfile onto a floppy and leave it at a trusted friends or relatives.  Might come in handy later. <P>Raydar, you're not being cynical, you're being pragmatic.  Big difference. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Get a lawyer. Now. Cover your ass.<BR>I apologize if this sounds cynical, but she's already proven that she can't be trusted. I wouldn't put anything past her. Time for you to look out for yourself. <BR>Go see a marriage counselor. I'm sure you need someone to talk with. <P>Maybe say a prayer? <BR>That's up to you. Works for me.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||11
Z||000063||Ken Wittlief||06-01-2001||11:01 AM||||If you are still going through this alone I second the recommendation from several posts up - give these folks a call:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.calvarychapel.org" TARGET=_blank>www.calvarychapel.org</A> <P>easy going people there - you are gonna need someone to lean on to get back on your feet. ||||reg||1
Z||000064||Broken_hearted||06-02-2001||01:38 PM||||I changed the lockes a couple days ago. I talked to her that same night and told her I did this. She seemed to not be too upset with it, but I was talking to her on AIM and couldn't see her face. When I got home yesterday, she wasn't here and I knew she hadn't been. For some reason that made me feel better... maybe because I knew she couldn't talk to him on my computer. Then about an hour after I got home she pounds on the door. I let her in as she storms through the house - she was very mad. She started gathering more clothes and telling me this is still her house too. I never raised my voice and told her that I wasn't locking her out forever and her stuff would be safe here, I wasn't going to harm her stuff. I sat on the bed as she gathered some things and I tried to talk her down from this rage she was in. I hated to know that she was mad at me. Eventually she sat down on the bed with me and we started to talk. I told her I miss her, but when I come home and she is here, I know she doesn't want to see me. Then I feel uncomfortable in my own house. Then I asked her is she misses me. She said, "yeah, the old you." I told her the old me is back (romantic and not such a bone-head), but she refuses to think I won't change again. I asked her what she missed about me. She told me she misses when I would walk in the door I would smile and say hi. If I was outside working on my car or truck or whatever, I would walk in and out several times in a short period. Every time I walked in I would tell her "Hi". Just a little thing I did. She would smile and say hi back - I miss that, too. She told me several things she missed about me. Soon we both were in tears. I crawled up next to her and said under my breathe, "I do love you, I swear!" She could only hear this because I was right next to her ear, and then she really started to cry. This I don't understand: It's like she is trying so hard to leave me, like she is forcing this to happen. Something inside just tells me this isn't right. We talked about our times together and the tears just wouldn't stop.<P>I didn't want this to end, so I asked her if she wanted to go do something. She said, "Like what?" I told her anything she wanted to do is fine with me. We ended up going out to eat and to see a movie. We saw Pearl Harbor. The love story in that movie brought tears to my eyes. That sort of thing I never really payed any attention to, until now. During the movie she didn't reach for my hand like she used to and I didn't reach for hers. At one point her knee started to hurt and she put her leg on mine to stretch it out. I masseged her knee until my hand started to cramp, but I didn't stop. My leg fell asleep from her being there, but I didn't move it or tell her. The pain was nothing compared to her touch. Toward the end of the movie she was crying and I put my hand on her leg to let her know I was there. I was hoping she would grab my hand or something - she didn't. When the movie was over we sarted to walk out. I put my arm around her and then she put her arm around me. I know she was just being nice or whatever, but it felt so good to me, I pretended we were still happy, and for a brief second, I was floating.<P>When we got to my truck I opened her door as she got in. When we got home I wanted her to stay, so I asked her if she would be okay driving home, it was 12:30. She said she would be fine, but I was hoping she would stay. I know I'm fooloing myself by letting this go on the way it is. But I can't help but think that somehow this might not happen if I try hard enough. As she got in her car to leave I crouched down at her door. I looked in her eyes and told her I had a wonderful time - my chin started to quiver. I then got close to her (not to kiss her even though she will kiss me if I want). Then I whispered in her ear, "Jennifer, I will always love you. My love for you will never die, until I do. That I swear to you." I then closed her door and walked inside. It took my about 4 seconds to start missing her. <p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000065||JohnnyK||06-02-2001||01:53 PM||||You are going insane. Stop trying to hard. Your just making it harder on yourself (well, thats what it seems like from my point of view).||||reg||1
Z||000066||Mach10||06-05-2001||09:44 PM||||Still hangin' in there, broken_hearted?<BR>Hang tough, buddy.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000067||Broken_hearted||06-06-2001||11:35 PM||||Still nothing good to tell. I wish I could come here saying she wants to come back and make this work, but I guess it doesn't mean anything to her now. I guess I should have taken the advise I was given towards the beginning of this thread. Maybe I tried too hard. She says I tried too hard, like I was a fraud. I'm sure if I hadn't tried so hard she would have said, "You didn't try hard enough." It's a double edge sword... and it's right through my heart. I know this happens all the time around this world, but I don't see how people recover from it... like it never happened. Maybe the fact that she is leaving me for another guy is what hurts so much.<P>I have so much to say, but can't find a way to put it into words for you to read.<P>I guess i'm hanging in there, or maybe hanging myself. Every day she gets madder at me about not helping her get the divorce under way. We never argued, until this started. Now all we(mostly her) do is get mad and start yelling. <P>Life sucks right about now...<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000068||MsBeth||06-07-2001||12:15 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Broken_hearted:<BR><B>I know this happens all the time around this world, but I don't see how people recover from it... like it never happened. Maybe the fact that she is leaving me for another guy is what hurts so much.<P><BR>Life sucks right about now...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Broken_hearted,<P>It does get better ... I just had this happen to me - within the last year.  After 7 years together.  It wasn't easy and I still have a bad day now and again but with the help of friends and family - I got through and found my life better than I ever could imagine.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I am really sorry for your pain- sadly I remember it well.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>MsBeth||||reg||9
Z||000069||mbramble||06-07-2001||08:58 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>....gets madder at me about not helping her get the divorce under way...<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What does she expect you to do to help out??  All she needs to do is get a lawyer and file for divorce.  Has she even done that??  Or is she expecting you to take care of all of it for her??  Have you talked with a lawyer yet??  You really should....<p>[This message has been edited by mbramble (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000070||Raydar||06-07-2001||09:43 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mbramble:<BR><B>  Or is she expecting you to take care of all of it for her??  Have you talked with a lawyer yet??  You really should....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As painful as this is, you need to get a lawyer. If you let her make all the moves, she's going to use that to her advantage. You already have evidence that she's slept with somebody else. Right? USE IT! Cover yourself! She's already said she wanted the house. If you don't do anything, that just may happen. <BR>Look out for your own hide. She's sure as hell not going to. <BR>You will get past this. But you can't bury your head in the sand and hope it'll go away. It's pretty clear that it won't.<BR>Also, you should find a minister or counselor to talk with.   <P>I also will add... If you happen to hook up with the old g/f, DO NOT sleep with her (or anybody else) until this is resolved. You don't want to give your wife anything to use against you.<BR>Sorry. I'm ranting. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000071||JohnnyK||06-07-2001||12:42 PM||||Good point Raydar. Go freakin do something about it! It won't go away, and your going to get raped (excuse the term) in the whole settlement.||||reg||1
Z||000072||thomas_l||06-07-2001||04:00 PM||||When I split with my ex, I was the one that initiated, but after some months she was the one that decided it was time for a divorce "to get closure". We agreed on terms, I went and paid a paralegal $50 to draw up the necessary docs, then I went to the clerk of the courts and filed for about $35. A month later (30 day wait here in GA with uncontested divorce & no kids), I went back and got a judge to sign the order and it was done. I elected to get it done myself because I knew she was bad about not getting things completed or following through properly. I took care of business and got it done mydamnself.<P>On the way home with the freshly signed divorce I was crying inside and out. I never felt more empty in my life. After a while tho, there was a sense of relief. I wasn't liable for anymore of her credit shenanigans, I didn't owe her anything, and I was free and clear to get my own $hit together.<P>Keep your chin up and take care of business right now before someone else takes care of it for you.||||reg||1
Z||000073||Ken Wittlief||06-07-2001||05:04 PM||||This may sound unpleasent<P>if you remember back to your wedding day<P>and the vows you and your wife made to each other<P>to me, thats what a marriage is - keeping only to each other, forsaking all others.<P>when she was with another guy, from my way of looking at it, the marriage ended right there. Everyone has disagreements and problems and fights and stuff<P>but when you get into bed with someone else, you have just ended you marriage.<P>  all thats left now is the paperwork. (in my opinion anyway)<P>  If you are still thinking you want to pull this back together, then you need to get it into your head that the marriage is already over - if you want her back you have to compete against someone else now for her affection.<P>   that totally sucks. Not a good place to be for your feelings or for your dignity.<P>  I hate to say it, but if she has feelings for someone else, and has already acted on them, its time to let her go.<P>  you may very well fell there will never be anyone else for you<P>  and you may decide to not start up a relationship with anyone else<P>  and maybe someday she will realize she has made a mistake, and will want to come back to you<P>   its your decision where you go from here.<P>But for right now you and her need to face up to the fact that this marriage is already over. <P>  Love can only be given and received by a free spirit. Your wife (ex wife?) has to know she is free to go if she wants to.<P>  If you want to keep the porch lite on and let her know, if she ever changes her mind then she if free to come back too, thats up to you.<P>   dont rush into it. give yourself some time to stop hurting. Once you get your head back above water you may find some other things you want to focus your life on<P>  maybe even some things you couldnt do when you were married.<P>  my parents were divorced. my mom went out right away with some guy that had a part in the breakup. He turned out to be a slimball. Then she met a great guy and got married again.<P>   my dad also remarried, but that didnt work out and they divorced after a few years. <P>  after about 6 years, my moms second husband died suddenly of a stroke. It was rough on her.<P>  about a year later both my parents were at someone house for a party or something, now they were both alone again, they had both been through a lot, they had both learned a lot<P>  and I could almost see them getting back together, it could of worked out.<P>  they didnt, but the point is, you never know where life is gonna take you.<P>  So no matter how hard things are for you now, hold onto your character, continue to stand for what you believe in. As someone once put it, act in a manner that would make your grandmother proud of you.<P>  Someday you are gonna be telling your kids about your 1st marriage, or your friends, or your new family. You want to be able to look back on this and be confident that you handled the difficult situation well.||||reg||1
Z||000074||DJRice||06-07-2001||09:07 PM||||You <B><I>really, REALLY</B></I> need to take the initiave to get the paperwork started for the Divorce now, while she is the one 'cheating' and you are still in the innocent.  Just because you are divorced doesnt mean your relationship has to be over for ever, but you have to protect yourself now or you could get screwed even worse down the line.<P>Protect yourself.  Its going to hurt a bit, but for heaven's sake give her what she wants and protect yourself in the process.  It's been said that divorce is latin for "ripping a mans genitals out thru his wallet".  Get this done while she is willing to let you get it started otherwise things can still continue to spiral downward.  You have been given great advise here on the forum, take it......||||reg||1
Z||000075||Ken Wittlief||06-08-2001||09:03 AM||||Yes brokenheart - its time to get your butt in gear<P> or do we have to come over there and kick you in the a$$<P>  there is a proverb that says something to the effect that a good kick it the butt from your friends is good for you<P>  but a kiss from your enemies brings death.<P>  in other words, your friends will force you to do the difficult but necessay things, but the people that dont care about you will tell you what you want to hear.<P>  time to start covering your behind.||||reg||1
Z||000076||ferg||06-08-2001||03:39 PM||||I've been reading all the posts here, and I have some stuff to add.<P>A couple of years ago, I was involved with this girl, we lived together when I was in school. We were at different points in our lives, I was a Junior in college and she had just moved to my state, and this was her first time being on a college campus. (we were both the same age) I had gone through all of the late night weekday partying, getting drunk all the time, hanging out w/ friends and stuff during my freshman year. She on the other hand went from high school to moving out on her own, living the adult life. When she moved in with me, she started doing the things that I did as a freshman. It worked for a while, but eventually our relationship and me, took a back seat to her new *freinds* (drinking buddies). She got infatuated with a guy at work, all I heard about was how great this guy was and stuff. I knew the guy, and he's actually an ok guy, sorta. We ended up breaking up on our 2 year anniversary. We had talked a lot about saving our relationship and what-not, we basically came to the conclusion that there was nothing left. She and I both knew it.<P>We broke up and 5 minutes later decided to get some dinner together. It was great, that dinner was the best time I had with her in almost a year. The pressure was off and we were both happy. I actually thought that we might get back together. After our dinner we went back to the apartment, and she went out for the night- not coming back until 3 or 4am. I was crushed, all I could think about was her with this guy. I got upset and tried to go to bed.<P>For the next couple of weeks, we talked everyday. I was hoping she had changed her mind and wanted to get back together, the more we talked the more she wanted to leave. <P>Anyway, I got really sh*tty to her, ruining any chance of getting back with her. I didn't care, because if she told me she slept with the new guy, I could not/ would not ever get back together with her after doing that. <P>We cut all ties and within a month I met the girl I am currently dating. We've been together for over 2 years and I've never been happier. Technically my gf is the "rebound" girl, but it makes no difference to me, I had found a girl I wanted to be with and that was that. <P>So broken- here's my advise to you. Get professional legal advice. No matter what condition your marriage is in, you are still in a contract with your wife(ex) so anything she does, you are equally responsible for. If she goes out and puts $10,000 on the joint credit card, you are just as responsible to pay it as she is. <P>Addionally, I've heard that it takes about 6 months to "get over" a relationship, once you've stopped contact with her. Once you say goodbye to her, don't have contact with her for over 6 months. <P>I also think, you should get this idea of 'getting back together' out of your head. She doesn't want to be with you. <P>Don't talk to her, that just re-opens the wounds, when you do have to see her, keep it on a professional level. Don't ask her how she's doing, don't tell her about you. Simply say- what do you want- here it is- goodbye. All you are doing is tearing yourself up by bringing up the same old stuff. You know how she feels, leave it alone.<P>On a side note- my freinds boss had a spat with his wife, she wanted to leave. She packed her bags and left while he was at work. So he called all of the credit card companies and cancelled her cards. He closed out the bank account, and since the car was in his name, he reported it stolen. Needless to say, she called him rather upset. He told her 'if you can do it without me fine- you get nothing' he also changed the locks the day he came home and he also removed her name from receiving any money upon his death. <P>She was begging to come back within a day.<P>Basically- cut her off from all your money.<BR>don't talk about your FORMER relationship.<BR>don't talk about a future relationship.<BR>don't talk to her.<P>What you are going through is rough, almost everyone has been there. Give yourself time to be alone. I know you don't think you'll ever find someone better, bla bla bla. I thought the EXACT same thing. Everyone told me the same thing I'm telling you- It does get easier with time, it will take a long time to be 100%, trust what the people here are telling you- they've been through it before and everyone knows exactly how you are feeling. <P>I helped myself get over the former girlfriend by thinking to myself- 'I'm going to get a great job have tons of money and have a great life very soon- all she has to look forward to is her white-trash life, and her white-trash friends, she's got no education and no real friends. If I ever see her again, she's going to kick herself for letting me go.' <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000077||GOLDSE||06-08-2001||06:06 PM||||As a new member here who has followed this post and been down the path of Divorce more than once...I can tell you that what the others are telling you is the truth.<P>I went through this same thing almost five years ago.  I thought the world would end, especially when the locks were changed on me and I was forced to live somewhere else.  Not being able to spend my time with my twin daughters was the most agonizing pain I have ever experienced.  <P>Let me tell you what worked for me.  FRIENDS.  I was lucky enough to have friends who would take the time to listen to my troubles.  They would be there whenever I needed them, rain or shine.  I can tell you they sure did get tired of hearing me talk about it, but they never told me.  I started hanging around my friends whom I hadn't been able to see much beforehand.  Being with these people and having a good time kept my mind off of the entire situation.  <P>The other thing that helped me out was RUNNING.  I got involved in an activity that took my mind off of my troubles.  When I ran I wasn't thinking about anything.  My mind was totally clear.  <P>You need to heed the advise of others in this thread.  You NEED to be able to speak to someone for your own personal support.  Talk to a professional counselor or your clergy.  You NEED to speak with a lawyer to determine your rights.  <P>I feel for you, and trust me life does get better.  I am now remarried (yes, the third time's a charm) to a wonderful woman and my life has never been better.  I have a new 3 month old son and a 5 year old stepson.  ||||reg||1
Z||000078||Broken_hearted||06-10-2001||08:40 PM||||I try very hard to forget her, but it's useless. This house is filled with her. Everything I see in this house, has some kind of memory of her attatched to it. And I love thinking about her, and us. Not sure how or if I will get past this... I will learn to go on without her. I have no choice.<P> :::SIGH:::||||reg||1
Z||000079||TWEETIE BIRD||06-10-2001||09:26 PM||||I have been reading your sad story since the beginning. What you had was a parent child <BR>relationship. You told her how it was and that was it. She grew up and wanted her wings. Children need <BR>to leave the nest, she is ready. What you lost was only a dream. It will never work out if you are always there. Find other interests, <BR>not other women. You will only be using them right now. If your house has bad memories, find somewhere else to live and don't take anything that reminds you of her. This all may sound cruel but it is reality. This all will make you a better person and when the next one comes along you will know what happiness is because you will know how to really love someone. You will be friends, lovers, companions and soul mates because you have learned from your mistakes and know how to appreciate each other.<BR>One last thing, if you do by some chance, get back together, you will never trust her again no matter how hard you try and this just <BR>doesn't ever work out. It would be like living in a prison for her. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000080||Mach10||06-10-2001||10:05 PM||||B_H: You are completely right. At the moment, it's quarter-to the end of the world, but--this is important--  it isn't THE end of the world yet. Sounds to me like you are finally starting to clear your mind. It's still going to be hard, but it seems to me that you're progressing.<P>Good to see that you're still with us.<BR>Keep in touch!||||reg||14
Z||000081||84Bill||06-11-2001||10:17 AM||||been there done that. I see this thread is a month or so old but still.<BR>When your in a relationship that long and your partner leaves you it's as if a family member has died. Even worse is the fact that they have not been burried and can still hang just out of your reach. So close and yet so damn far away. <BR>As hard as it sounds let her go to Reno let there be seperation because it is just prolonging the agony. you need to be away from her despratly so you can start the mourning process. It's so very hard I know. just cry alot because thats the best you can do. Talk alot because that the next best you can do. Just mourn the loss. Cry, yell at pictures of her, Cry and most of all look to others for help mom dad any family members. There is no fault, no one to blame especially not yourself. <BR>Write some poetry about her and and read it to yourself and just let it all out. Call it an epogy if you like. DONT GO DRINKING if you do your just putting it off and the hurt will last much longer. She is in pain too and she is hurting JUST as bad as you BUT she has a distraction and unfortunatly you don't so you need to distract yourself from her. You are going to be ok it just takes time a lot of pain and a river of tears. You are going through changes and now is the time to get into new things like hobbies and such. Learn how to FLY a plane or go sailing with a friend or two. just do the things that are different and do a lot of them Don't worry about being happy because your not. <BR>You are depressed, RECOGNISE that! know that your depressed tell people your depressed but be carefull depression is dangerous.  Just know that ok? Most people kidd themselves and end up loosing it and doing stupid stuff. You seem to be rather level headed about this mess atleast thats better than most. Your idea to put up this post was a good one.<BR>After a few months when your ready, make a resolution not to be unhappy any more.( your over the shock now) Fight the urge to think about her, take all the pictures and stuff and put them all in a box and "burry" it in your closet somewhere.  Get over that depression!<BR>You are ok now, laugh again smile more be happy more. It's ok to be upset about your loss but resolve not to let it dominate your every thought.<BR>Hang in there bub you'll be ok. I know your pain.<BR>Bill<P>If you want someone you can chat with I'm always around. wh_stewart@hotmail.com my MSN messanger ID is Boonyer. My ICQ is 27078941<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000082||84Bill||06-11-2001||11:52 AM||||Just store this away in the back of your mind.<P>All things happen for a reason. <P>Just because you don't know why doesn't mean you must find out. Just accept the fact that there is one. Someday down the road it will hit you and you will be like WOW what a long strange trip it's been, You may even smile. Trust me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000083||Broken_hearted||06-12-2001||12:28 AM||||I need to make something clear - She hasn't slept with this guy or seen him for 10 years or so.<P><B>The End</B><P>later....<p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000084||84Bill||06-12-2001||01:05 AM||||Look if you both really want to work it out get Men are from mars Women are from Venus You will be quite surprised at what you will learn. It's not JUST YOU OR HER it's BOTH OF YOU. read it and you'll see maybe you two need this little shake up. I went thru 2 years of total seperation and we got back together. It all started with talking to each other, listening and a boatload of forgiveness. Breaking up is hard. talking to each other is easy if you know how.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000085||Broken_hearted||06-12-2001||01:38 AM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000086||JohnnyK||06-12-2001||01:49 AM||||After reading the last 2 pages, i have to say GET OVER IT!. It's not ****ing going to work out for you. Life gave you a crack on the head. It happens to the best of us. At least ****ing TRY to get over it. Make an effort. Everybody goes through something like this. I'm sounding harsh, yes, but you ask for advice, and then you try the same old "I tried to get her back" thing. You sound like you REALIZE it's not going to happen, so make an effort for us! This was not meant to be harsh, I just couldn't think of a way to write it besides like this.<BR>Do NOT misinterpret this message though. I cannot even BEGIN to imagine what you are feeling. I couldn't even stand losing a girlfriend of 1.5 years to another guy. However, I DID eventually realize there is nothing I could do.<p>[This message has been edited by JohnnyK (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000087||Broken_hearted||06-12-2001||01:50 AM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000088||Mach10||06-23-2001||08:42 PM||||Still there, big guy?||||reg||14
Z||000089||Broken_hearted||06-23-2001||11:35 PM||||I was going to let this go and try to move on. But it's not getting any easier.<P>I actually talked to Jennifer today. All I wanted to do is hear her voice, but as soon as she got a word in, it was, "I've this all before... it's getting old. I wasn't trying to convince her to come back... just talking about the old times. She's so cold now. She has become the type of person I hate. I have even tried to hate her, but it's useless. <P>I managed to get in the Fiero and drive up one of the canyons. Actually smiled - I think. That car is the best thing that has ever happened to me. It has never let me down and never left me for another  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>If things like this are supposed to make you stronger, I should be strong enough to lift a freaking freight train! Too much of this kind of crap has beat me down my whole life, but this is the worst.<P>Oh well... life will get better. It can't get any worse because if it did, I would be dead....<P>||||reg||1
Z||000090||87GTBro||06-24-2001||12:57 AM||||Hey man... My heart goes out to you.  Sometimes stuff happens to people and you can't really explain why or how or anything like that.  Sometimes bad stuff happens to good people.<P>That being said, the worst thing you can do is think about the woulda, shoulda, couldas.<P>I have read this post almost from the beginning to the end.  I never posted a reply because, quite frankly, I don't think you would listen to a word I have to say.  I can't totally sympathize with you or even know how you are feeling because I am single and have never been married.<P>But I do know that, no matter how you might feel, it is not the end of the world.  If she is treating you like this, then the sad reality is that she never REALLY loved you in the first place.  Love is not something that comes and goes.  Love is eternal.  Conditional love is not love at all.  You would be closer if you called it Lust.<P>Try to find the bright side of all of this.  You have more time to do the things you always wanted to do, but you couldn't because you had to "check with the wife".  You can talk to other girls without being afraid of your wife seeing you or finding out and accusing you of being unfaithful.  You can spend more time with your car (which is what I like to do because cars don't complain or talk back)<P>I can guarrantee you will be more picky and selective the next time you meet someone.  There are other fish in the sea.  I guarrantee this is not the last time you will love.||||reg||1
Z||000091||Mach10||06-24-2001||01:48 PM||||Well, with that in mind, I guess we should stop pestering you then. It's good to hear that you're moving on. Having a little fun in a little fun car sounds like a good few steps. Keep it up. I'll stop harrassing you now. If you feel the need to talk to us, just resurrect this thread.<BR>Hang tough!||||reg||14
Z||000092||JohnnyK||06-24-2001||04:44 PM||||On the bright side, you haven't had a gunshot wound to the head go terribly wrong and have had to learn how to tie your shoelaces all over..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000093||Broken_hearted||06-24-2001||09:25 PM||||<B>All things happen for a reason.</B><P><B>...she never REALLY loved you in the first place.</B><P>Those two statements make sense. I have been talking to my highschool girlfriend for a while now. I didn't contact her until I knew my marriage was over. Susan is one of the sweetest people I know, and if I didn't have to move away from her, we would have ended up married. Now we might have a second chance. Maybe this will come out even better for me in the long run. To tell you the truth, Susan is a better person than Jennifer anyway.<P> I never thought Jennifer would have this type of person hidden somewhere, but she does. And for me to see this person makes me angry. It's like one day everything was fine... then all hell breaks loose. I love Jennifer more than anything to this day! I will love her til the day I die! But she loves another guy... not me. That's hard for me to figure out, but then again, it doesn't matter if I can figure it out or not. It's not my choice, and that makes me feel totally helpless. <P>The hardest part about all this is being alone, I have never really been alone in my life. Just as soon as I moved out of my family's house to my own apartment, Jennifer moved in with me. Good thing I have my faithful dog to help me through this. <P>Still have half an hour until I call Susan.. just burning up time.<P>Keeping my fingers crossed....||||reg||1
Z||000094||LarryB||06-24-2001||09:48 PM||||Here's hoping the phone call goes well...||||reg||14
Z||000095||Broken_hearted||06-25-2001||11:42 PM||||The phone call to Susan went well... they always do.<P>I originally came here to explain to Jennifer why I think Susan is a better person than her. (Jennifer has found this thread) Today she expresses her feelings to me about me saying Susan is a better person than her. I deleted it all. No point in beating a dead horse...<P>After being with someone for almost 9 years, going through what we have been through and pulling through somehow... just seems stupid to throw it all away. I just never thought anything could tear us apart.<p>[This message has been edited by Broken_hearted (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000096||BAM-BAM||06-26-2001||11:55 PM||||  Broken_hearted. Listen man, the pain will pass and you'll feel better. Just check this thread every day for all the support you'll need. There's only one way to recover though bro; Country Music. I don't care if you normally listen to it or not, it will help. I have two dedications for you an I want you to listen to them. <P>Dedication #1) to Jennifer, any Sam Kennison album ever made. That was simple<P>Dedication #2) To Susan, Clay Walkers new song "If you ever feel like loving me again". <P>Now I don't know who wrote that song but it has to be one of the greatest songs I have ever heard. It will all fit together when you hear it.<BR> Listen, Who knows why these things happen, read the post on Sex in a Fiero (page three) and you'll know where I am coming from. People, for some unknown reason, Just up and change. Trying to figure it out and place blame is an imposibility. She needed something else and you have no control over that. What you do have control over is the rest of your life. Like Lisa said, love yourself first. The rest will follow.  <BR>   Seriously though, listen to that song, it will change your heart every time you think of Susan. Oh, one more thing. Don't be to quick to jump back in, you have issues to work out now. Get them under control and slow your roll. It takes time bro. Hope I wasn't to harsh.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P> If you ever feel like loving me again<BR> The doors wide open baby walk right in<BR> I will love you till the end of time<BR> Heart and mind<BR> Just like I did back then<P> If you ever feel like needing some one<BR> Who will love you like Iv'e alwasy done<BR> I will show you some things never end<BR> If you ever feel like loving me again<p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 06-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000097||JohnnyK||06-27-2001||02:31 AM||||Broken: I don't quite understand your last post? You thought that susan was a better person and now you changed your mind? Explain (I could have the meaning totally wrong, I just don't understand what your saying?)||||reg||1
Z||000098||gyro1s||06-27-2001||03:44 AM||||two things <BR>1. Good counsellor<BR>2. good lawyer<BR>counsellor to help you get your head on straight, lawyer to cover your a$$.<BR>worked for me.<BR>good luck, <BR>jeff ||||reg||1
Z||000099||Broken_hearted||06-27-2001||10:25 PM||||What I meant to say was: Jennifer has changed. The new Jennifer is not such a good person in my eyes. Don't get me wrong... I love her to this day, and I know I will always love her. She stopped loving me years ago according to her, but I never saw it. She went way out of her way to make sure I didn't see it, or she is making it up to make herself feel better. At one point, when she was still living here, she asked me if she could go see him to "see if there is anything there". I blew up for the first time ever in our marriage. I hit the counter so hard, I thought I broke my hand, then I hit it again and said some stupid things. I have never done that before, never made myself seem so mean, but damn... that hurt like hell. Those words made me feel like I was dead... maybe my heart skipped a beat or two. If that wasn't enough, a few days later, Jennifer got two days off work to have a four day weekend. We are still married, still living together and she informs me that she <B>is</B> going to see him. The only reason she didn't go - I took all the keys to our vehicles while she was packing her bags. <P>I guess I better throw this in because Jennifer reads this thread. I changed too. Over the years I stopped opening her door to the car when we went places. I stopped kissing her. I stopped doing the little things that mean <B>everything</B>. But I never stopped loving her. Oh well... that's starting to change. <P>Our marriage means nothing to her and everything to me. Isn't that backwards? I guess at one point Jennifer thought it meant nothing to me, but over the years I guess I let some things get in the way. I made some stupid mistakes over the years, but I NEVER strayed. Maybe it just happens to everyone that has been married for that long... something just fades away. It can't be like it was when we first met forever, it just can't. Now that she has a new interest, maybe it feels like when we (or they) first met. This may sound cruel, but I hope he isn't what she hoped. I mean... they were in highschool when they last saw each other. Now she tells me she will be happy because he loves her. He does? And now when i ask her if she thinks I love her, she says I don't. I don't? <P>I'm rambling again. I guess it all comes down to this: I thought we were forever. When I said, "I do", I meant it forever. For better or for worse.<P>I don't plan on jumping into another realationship anytime soon... no way! <P>||||reg||1
Z||000100||JSocha||06-28-2001||10:26 AM||||Broken Hearted.<P>Been following this thread everytime I see that somebody has been posting to this but have not been responding.<P>You keep telling us Jennifer has not only found this thread, but is reading it.<P>Therefore, one, how do we know what you are telling us is is not just a way to get a message across to Jennifer and using the forum as support.<P>Two, you may as well forget Jennifer, find a good lawyer, cover your backside like others have suggested in the meantime, because no matter how much "you" love her, she is never going to reciprocate that love back and there is no way of changing a woman's mind once it is made.<P>I realize this is hard.  Been there myself and I see from my perspective and past experience what is going on.<P>I'm sure you have heard of "Tough Love".  Don't know you personally, but if that is what it is going to take to help you out of this rutt "she" has put you in, then that is the response you will be getting from me, Tough Love in nature.<P>You need to start working on forgetting her.  If she is still in the house, living there, she has pretty much stated she doesn't need you, therefore, either kick her @$$ out or pack your things, find a different place to live and get the heck out and leave her with the bills to the old place.<P>IMO, she is trying to live both worlds and step off the boat when she is ready to do so when it is convinent for her, leaving you there to sink and drown in misery without a second thought about you.<P>And if your always playing the good guy part, she is just going to suck that up until you are nothing but a used up corpse to throw to the way side when she is ready.<P>This is not healthy for you!  You have a lot of friends here, including me that can be your positive support versus the negative support you are getting from her.<P>Now, if your immidate reaction to this was to post a "But you do not understand" or act in her defense in any way shape, form or matter, then you would be immidately proving my point entirely as to what you are doing and what she is doing to you.<P>We will be here for you.  I am going to lay it straight as I have been down the path, not marriage wise, but being with and living with g/f, which is equivalent to being married, just no licence.<P>Others that have been down this path, are more then likely seeing the same thing I am.<P>The old adatage, "You can not con a con" comes to mind is relavent here.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000101||RiceCooker||06-28-2001||11:00 AM||||We're all with you on this one BH. <P>I know I'm still young but i have been in a tough situation like this as well. I lost the woman I was going to marry. When a woman changes her mind about the person she loves or is interested in, it does not change back and that person does indeed seem like an entirely different human being. I can sympathise with you on difficult it actually is.<P>Everyone says, "Time will Heal you wounds" and "it will pass" but the trouble is time moves so slowly and you want to just hibernate for 6 months and hope to feel better. Just keep yoourself busy with things (even the smallest things) that make you happy.<P>I have a couple poems (yes you can be a fiero lover and still have a sensitive side) that may make you feel better.<P>Take it easy BH<P><BR>ALIVE<BR>---------<P>What is this sound, this awakening noise?<BR>Have I forgotten my strength, my poise?<BR>It is much too loud, this cry in my head<BR>Could I not have heard it? Was I dead?<BR>All too often I have hit the snooze<BR>Will it ever heal? This substantial bruise?<BR>For the bruise is deep and the scars are thick<BR>The process is tough and it will not be quick<P>I must regain the will, the ultimate drive<BR>To be fair to myself, to be Alive<P>Though I will be back, it will not be soon<BR>I must first realize what problems have loomed<BR>The way out of this deadly quicksand<BR>Is to realize why, to understand<BR>For I know now, but I did not then<BR>Who, what, how, where, why and when<BR>The path to my heart blocked the path to my mind<BR>I must use these together, I am beginning to find<P>A beautiful body and mind, for this I will strive<BR>My heart and soul will never feel more Alive<P>A great lesson learned, much too cliché<BR>Is that good things will go, while great things will stay<BR>Some things are so great, we must not forget<BR>Life is far too short to feel regret<BR>Changes are made the door opens wide<BR>The invitation is given, time to step inside<P>A bright star on the horizon has been sighted<BR>The passion is present, the fire ignited<BR>We must not forget, no matter how great the bribe<BR>We must reach deep inside to feel the vibe<P>The dreams are the inspiration to the drive<BR>The goals are for what we strive <BR>We will overcome, we will thrive<BR>For everyday, we are so ALIVE<P><BR>GLOW<BR>--------<P>Memories so vivid, and how the dreams they tease<BR>Smells & sounds & touch & taste of lost eternities<BR>Twists & turns, this road of life, tires often squeal<BR>While passing cars reveal the scars, of a pain that is surreal<P>Save it for a rainy day<BR>For today the sky is bright<BR>All the pain of yesterday<BR>Must not win the fight<P>Impossible it seems, to achieve a brand new start<BR>The truth CAN be realized, once the eyelids part<BR>Passion & beauty remain, strong as ever <BR>As warm summer nights last forever<P>Save it for a rainy day<BR>For tonight the stars shine bright<BR>All the smiles of yesterday <BR>Have shaped a beautiful life<P>The search continues, regardless of the sting<BR>Completely unaware of what tomorrow brings<BR>No need to get anxious, no need to be blue<BR>For those who believe, wishes do come true<P>Save it for a rainy day<BR>For our hearts, our lives burn bright<BR>All the dreams of yesterday<BR>May come true tonight<P>||||reg||3
Z||000102||Broken_hearted||06-29-2001||05:06 PM||||I stopped posting when I found out Jennifer had started reading the thread... I thought it would influence what I posted. <P>Jennifer doesn't live here anymore, and doesn't want to take me to the cleaners. She is willing to give me anything I want, including the house. I want to keep the house, but at the same time I am not sure I want to be "stuck" here until it sells if I choose. Then again, I don't want to run off and stick her with everything. Like I told Jennifer: If it weren't for the third party invloved, I would leave the house, deposit my paychecks to her account and support her. I would even mow the lawn and leave the keys to the Fiero (she loves that car). That's not the case though. So here I am trying to decide what I am going to do in the near future, and trying to figure out how I am going to make it through this. It has been almost 3 months since she moved out and the tears keep coming. I know I'm not supposed to think back and wonder what might have been if only... But it's so hard to not do that.<P>Yesterday Jennifer came by(she doesn't stop by to see me... just to get some things). We had a few words that weren't very nice, but eventually I came to the conclusion: I don't want her to hate me. I was trying to make myself hate her... thinking it would help me let go. It wasn't working. So I decided to speak from my heart, trying to be reasonable. I know that she has made up her mind, and there is no way I can change it, but letting this mean side of me out won't make it any easier for either of us. I guess it worked because we calmed down and actually went out to see a movie (The fast and the Furious). Don't get me wrong... I did enjoy that time with her very much, and at the same time I kept telling myself that this is no hint that she wants to get back together. I also told myself that if we were to ever stand a chance of getting back together at any time down the road, I need to remind her of who I really am. I know it's a long shot for us to get back together in the future, but it's what I would want, so I am keeping that option open. We are meant to be together, and if she ever realizes that, I will welcome her back with open arms. Is that so bad?<P>I know I have been told many times to get a lawyer to cover my ass, but that's not a problem. Jennifer says she will take the house if I don't want it. She doesn't want my truck or my Fiero. She just wants me out of her life(ouch). Last night I told her I loved being with her, then she told me she loved being with me when I'm not trying to talk her into coming back. (Hmmm... lets see. She likes who I am when I'm not trying to get her back, and she loves being with me. So if she came back, I wouldn't be trying to get her back... life would be great! I know, I know, Jennifer, it was a joke... kind of)<P>Don't take this the wrong way people... I know it's over. Just putting my thoughts down into words the best I can (not doing a very good job of it though)<P>||||reg||1
Z||000103||Voytek||06-29-2001||05:42 PM||||You ARE doing a good job at expressing how you feel.  I think I know exactly what's going through your mind.  I believe you're doing a good thing by being more passive and I think she's made at least one right decision by not fighting over material possessions - nobody knows about the future.<BR>The only thing I would do (and that's just a personal opinion - I'm not in your shoes) is not to go out on any of these 'dates' anymore (like the movies).  IMHO all that does is remind you of the old times and makes it tougher to let her go (then again, you might feel the exact opposite).  Looks like you're keeping the doors open, anyways, and that's the best way.  Not one of us is perfect, some just have a bit more luck.<P>(edited for grammar)<BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 06-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000104||JohnnyK||06-29-2001||06:20 PM||||Yeah, you can't freakin progress if you keep taking her out to movies.. sheesh. you obviously weren't meant to be together, or else, well, you would be. Sorry dude||||reg||1
Z||000105||fierobaby||07-01-2001||01:45 AM||||well also being a teenager I am not the best at this whole relationship thing but I can join you in having a broken heart. I think I broke the heart of someone very important to me. I don't know what I would do with out him. he is the most perfect man and I hope we can work it out...but if not then maybe it is for the best...<P><BR>you will make it though and if you ever need a shoulder to cry on I am  here... but like everyone else said let her go... nothing can make her stay and if she does you will never be happy and neither will she you may think that making her stay will make you happy trut h is it won't you'll worry she is cheating on you and you'll worry if she is happy or not...<P>put yourself first... how about you go out to some hobby store...or craft store even and find something you would love to try maybe building a plane model or latch hooking something like that. Work on it when you are thihnking about her... it will help I promise I did that with my last boyfriend and I know you're thinking hun you're 17 how did you get your heart broken so young... well at the age of 14 you are impressinable. he was my first b/f and he broke my heart into a million peices.. thank god someone had some glue handy... I just hope there is some of that glue left... because I am going to need it<P>my prayers are with you and feel free to IM me on AIM fierobaby87<P><P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000106||DRH||07-02-2001||02:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Broken_hearted:<BR><B>Like I told Jennifer: If it weren't for the third party invloved, I would leave the house, deposit my paychecks to her account and support her. I would even mow the lawn and leave the keys to the Fiero (she loves that car). </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Forget the lawyer for now... See a counselor/therapist/whatever for yourself, not as a couple... You need to get some self-esteem, pronto! Loving someone does NOT mean being their doormat. It also does NOT mean you should be unable to be happy without them. You really need to talk to a trained professional about what is going on here.<P><BR>||||reg||1
