Z||000109||Sly88-GT||07-05-2001||10:56 AM||||Keep your chin up buddy. I'm new here, and your's is the first I'm responding to. ||||reg||1
Z||000110||Broken_hearted||07-16-2001||01:15 AM||||It's been about 4 months and 10 days since my wife let me know she loved another. It's been 3 months and 9 days since she moved out(also the day she told me she wanted a divorce). Now it's just a matter of days until her and her boyfriend are going to spend a week together in Reno. We are still married, but the marriage is over - her choice. It's nothing like knowing that you have been pushed aside for a guy that your wife has only spoken to on the phone and computer. After 9 years, you mean less to her than a voice or a keystroke!<P>I know I'm not supposed to remember old times, and I guess time is supposed to heal the hurt, but it won't erase the memories. And when I think of the memories, the pain is almost too much. Today I dusted off the Fiero and went for a spin. Wouldn't you know it... I passed the spot where we met. I tried to shrug it off until I passed the place we stopped for gas in my first Fiero the night we met. Then I decided to go home. And sure enough, an Ozzy Osborne song came over the radio - I only had 1 CD with me the night we met... Ozzy. Then I couldn't help but think more about when we first met and I remembered one night in paticular. We were at my house when I had to run to the store. On my way to the store I saw an old friend, and we ended up talking for about 15 minutes or so. When I finally got back, she ran to me in tears... she thought something happened to me. I didn't really think about it because I knew I was OK, but she didn't. I just held her until she felt better. It was then I knew the feeling - the feeling that somebody outside of my family really cared for me. From that moment on I took care to let her know where I was and I was OK. That's all gone now... she could care less where I go (and maybe that I am OK). It seems I am supposed to feel the same way... she lets me know this. How can that happen? How can you mean so much to somebody one day, and the next (actually 9 years later) you are just an obstical? That's how I feel, like something that is just in the way. I know I feel the same way about her (actually I feel even more). I care more about her than anything on this earth... dare I say, more than my own mother? The only answer to this is: she never loved me (wow, that really hurts). I mean, how can you fall out of love with someone that you actually loved enough to promise to spend the rest of your life with? It's like you stop caring for your own brother or something? You just stop? Even if there are mistakes made... you just stop? Just flip a switch? Some one tell me where that switch is, please.<P>I know I'm just churning up the coals that might be able to fade away if given a chance (I really don't believe that). It may not be mutual, but I really do care about Jennifer. And I <B>can't</B> help but love her. She has done the worst thing a married person can do - fall in love with another. Believe me, that hurts like hell, but it doesn't change the fact that I love her. <P>I have been reading up on divorce trying to find answers. I didn't really find any, but I did find a few facts. It seems women cope with divorce better than men (I had no clue, I figured the opposite). I also found out that women move on faster and are happier quicker than us (no kidding!). I have gone through a lot to try and get over her. I drove 29 hours to see my brother. All I wanted to do is talk about Jennifer. I was there for about two days before my head almost exploded and ended up driving home.<P>I guess I shouldn't have let somebody get so close to me. All the other people in my life I keep at a distance. I guess I feel like the less people I care about, the less I would get hurt. My dad was a drunk and my grandfather as well. My mom left my dad because of the booze, my grandma did the same to her husband. It really hurts when you're a kid and you remember your dad grabbing the table cloth and pulling it along with everything on the table. I won't go any farther than that. I also remember my grandpa coming home Christmas day to stumble and fall on my new electric train I got from Santa. I never understood why they just couldn't stop drinking. Then I reached the age of 16. The cool thing was to get smashed on the weekends. I did this for a few years until I realized something - I was drinking alone. By this time I had almost forgotten my grandpa and dad being drunks. But one night the memories just came flooding back and that night I swore to not turn out like them. Since that night I have not been drunk and might have had about 20 beers over a 12 year period. I have no clue where my dad is and haven't heard from him since that day my mom packed us up and hauled ass. He never tried to find us (as far as I know) and could probably care less. Right now I am 29 years old and life seems to be over. My family that lives in the same town as me (my aunt, grandma, 4 cousins, and my mom) all know that I am going through divorce, but none have contacted me to see how I am doing - besides my mom. Maybe the fact that I never let anyone get close to me is starting to haunt me now.<P>Happiness (May 14, 1994 - March 5, 2001)<P><BR>||||reg||9
Z||000111||Levi||07-16-2001||09:09 AM||||I'm really sorry you're feeling this way. And I do wish there was something I could do to help you or to tell you things will be alright. But I can't, no one can. <P>I have been reading this tread since it was started but did not reply. At the time that you posted it I was going to a pretty bad time myself. Nothing as serious as yours since it was my boyfriend that broke up with me and not my wife. And let me tell you reading other peoples replies and advice to you, I know I tried to follow it but it didn't work. It didn't make me feel any better. <P>I guess I never really believed that time heals all wounds. But you know what, it does you just have to wait it out and stick with it. <P>My favorite show (Sex and the City) says it takes half the amount of time you were with the person to get over them. And I know 4 and half years is a long time, and I do hope it doesn't take that long. For me it's been 4 months and even though I get sad a lot I'm ok and will be ok.<P>There was a time where I thought we'd always be together and even though that's still kind of hard to accept, now I realized that there will be others out there for me. In the last two weeks I have done more stuff (that I like doing and wanted to do) with a guy friend then I did with him. Because it was never anything he wanted to do. Like Bingo (hehe), and swimming and clubbing and golf (my first time, but it was fun). So I guess that has helped me a great deal, to know that even though I will always love him in one way (my ex) and he'll probable always be my bestfriend (we are trying to be friends, I'm kinda difficult but he's patient) that there will be others you'll have fun with again and others you'll enjoy spending your time with.<P>So my only advice to you is to stick it out, it will take time but keep sharing your feelings and thoughts. And all your pals here on the forum are there for you.<BR>And about not getting close to people. I don't either, I'm very afraid of getting hurt. But sometimes you have to take that chance, because it's the only way you'll get to know people, and people will get to know you.<P>Good luck, and keep posting once and a while to let us know how you're doing.||||reg||1
Z||000112||JSocha||07-16-2001||10:29 AM||||Nobody's asking you to forget.  <P>The point is...Don't Forget!<P>Remember "ALL" the good times you had and consider it just that, "The Good Times".<P>In a male tonal as well as imagery reference, think of it as the "notches" under the old belt.<P>Her, more then likely the notches on her bed post to come.  Sorry, couldn't resist (SLAP!)  Anywhooo, she made her bed, now let her sleep in it in total future misery as I know she will.<P>Cinderella's song comes to mind, "Don't Know What You Got Until Its Gone".  And that is exactly what SHE will have to face someday.<P>In the meantime, another song comes to mind, I think by Night Ranger (???) "Look Away".  And that is what you need to do!<P>Remembering the good times (and excuse me ladies who may also be following this for my cander about saying this, but I got them in bed first before the other Bozo's they eventually ended up with did) helped me out of the dumps many years back when my G/F of 3 years and who I had been living with, dumped me for one of my classmates (who promised her the world - Hardee!-Har!-Har! How it Tis to laugh just thinking of that!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ).  BTW, he is a drug-dependent alcholic, could care less about his son (as he is always being dropped off at my ex's mothers place) and cheats on her every chance he gets.  And she also "ballooned" out like a "Fat Little Moon Pig!" after she broke up with me and started dating him.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I seen her once 2 years back at the clinic and I said hi to her, but deep inside I told myself, I'm sure glad "I DIDN'T MARRY HER".  She has more roles then a bakery now!<P>Somehow, the "What comes around, goes around" just makes me happy every time I think of what he promised her (compared to the lifestyle I could have given her...ha!ha!ha!ha!ha!) and yet is doing to her (ahhhh! There must be a God who sided with me!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> )!<P>Anyways, put down my biography you seem to be reading from and sharing with the rest of us as I already know how it is going to end.<P>Believe it or not, "HAPPILY EVER AFTER!"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000113||Ken Wittlief||07-16-2001||10:52 AM||||I wish we had more than one word for LOVE in english - I think in Greek there are 5 or 6 different words for it.<P>  That thing that you go through when you are a teenager - that we call 'falling in love' is one thing<P>  but as I got older I realized there is something else, that we also call love - and its totally different<P>  'falling in love' is something that happens to you - its fleeting and it cannot endure<P>  real LOVE is something YOU DECIDE TO DO! it doesnt happen to you - and you have complete control over whether or not YOU decide to love someone<P>  real LOVE is:<P>  patient - waits for the other person to find their own pace - recognizes we all march to a different drummer.<BR>  kind - alway<BR>  never feeling jelious and never puts the other in a position to feel jelious<BR>  never allows you to walk around full of pride infront of the other<BR>  cannot be confinded - has no limits of time / space / or in what you are willing to do for your loved one - climb any mountain - cross any sea - reaches across infinite distance when you are apart...<BR>  always abides - you are always there for her when she needs you - nothing can keep you away.<P>  Hopefull - you never give up on the other person - you never write them off - always hope for the best from them and for them<P>  NOT BLIND! you see the other person as they really are - you see their strenths AND WEAKNESSES and you choose to continue to love them anyway - you dont pretend they are someone they are not.<P>  Faithful - you always trust and believe in that person - have faith in them, and you never do anything to cause them to loose their faith in you<P>  Eternal - even though our relationships with our spouses are until 'death do us part' the LOVE that we give takes on a life of itself - and it will exist forever. This also means LOVE is unconditional - no matter what illness / bad luck / mistakes you make / no matter how much the other person messes up - you still choose to love them forever.<P>   When I hear someone say they are leaving their spouse because "I dont love you anymore" as if some thing has happened to them that is beyond their control it makes me want to scream.<P>   A couple stands at the alter and promises to LOVE each other for the rest of their lives - if love is something that happens to you - if its beyond your control - them promising to love someone forever is an empty promise.<P>   But when people understand that real love is NOT that butterflys in the stomach thing that happens to schoolgirls - when you realize it is something you CHOOSE to do - then the promise "I will LOVE you forever" takes on a whole new meaning.<P>  not that I will cling onto some fleeting feeling that came over me in the past - but rather - every day - no matter what happens, no matter what we have to face or go through, I promise I will continue to choose to LOVE you - and there is nobody or thing in the universe that will ever take that away from us.<P>   The real thing that is behind this is the ability to see beyond the flesh and bones, the cirmunstances of our birth, the strengths and weaknesses we all have, our accomplishments and our failures, the fact that we all have an idea of what we want to do - and a separate bunch of stuff that we actually do<P>   LOVE sees beyond all this to the center of our being - to our souls - and realizes we are all precious - no matter who we are - we all need to be loved.<P>  Everything else pales in compairision.||||reg||1
Z||000114||JSocha||07-16-2001||11:18 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR><B>Now he has me even more profoundly confused then I already was!</B><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000115||Fierochic88||07-16-2001||01:06 PM||||Hey bud...keep your chin up!  Glad to know you're still with us but sorry to hear that your situation hasn't improved!  However, don't go by what you said about never letting someone get so close to you again.  Though this may sound out of place right now sooner or later it may have an effect on your life "Don't punish the fresh faces of your future because of what has been done to you by those in your past."  I am learning this lesson as we speak.  I have met a nice guy who has at times felt like I am behaving a certain way because of people who have hurt me in the past without giving him his own chance and he says that it really hurts him sometimes that I can't view him as a different person.  He is right and since I have been taking that lesson to heart things have been going a lot better.<P>I don't believe that your wife never loved you.  People can, and do, fall out of love with someone sometimes.  In the end, I guess it comes down to the fact that the love wasn't strong enough to endure or things happened along the way that cracked it.  Realizing this hurts but in the end this realization is better than the hurt you carry thinking she never loved you.  I was with my high school sweetheart nearly 4 years.  He had actually bought me an engagement ring (hadn't given it to me yet) when I decided to break things off.  It wasn't that I didn't care about him or didn't "love" him it was just that the relationship wasn't right for me (or for him, little did he realize) at that point in our lives.  He kept after me for nearly 6 months trying to work things out despite my insistence we were better off friends.  He insisted that I had never loved him and was a very cruel person.  Really I had loved him (and still in a way do) it's just that our love wasn't working the way it should and the course of our lives needed to be changed.  Now he has met a wonderful girl and has been with her for nearly 2 years.  He is very happy and the two of us frequently talk about how the end of our relationship worked out best for all parties concerned, despite the initial pain involved.  We can laugh and be friends and remember happy times together without any harsh feelings being exchanged and yes in my heart, I do still love him...but now as a dear friend.<P>Jennifer<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000116||Ken Wittlief||07-16-2001||03:33 PM||||Hey FieroChic88<P>  im not trying to imply that you didnt really love your old BF - of course you did - on the level that was appropriate for a BF/GF relationship.<P>  when you take the long walk down the isle you are taking it to another level - real love grows over the years - whole new levels occur that you dont even expect until it happens.<P>  thats what I was taking about when I mentioned the greek language has several words that we all summarize to be = to love. We use the same word for everything from our life partners to hot fudge sundays - we love everything and everyone.<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000117||AusFiero||07-18-2001||02:08 AM||||Ok I have read this a long time with no comment. It seems to boil dow to the fact she was the one starting to flirt with another guy regardless of her reasons then tried to reverse it by saying you was having an affair. That in itself proves she is a woman of low moral character that you are better off without.<P>Fine you love her but if she is capable of doing what she did you was never going to be happy and it was only a matter of time. She did not attempt to make an effort required to make a marriage work, better or for worse I think I heard in my wedding vows.<P>She is a quitter who bails on the worse. She will do this all her life, not just to you, to anybody. She has made her bed, let her lie in it and she will see how bad her life is probably going to turn out as she will leave this current guy for the next interesting flirt material she finds. Do not beleive the only guy she has slept with BS, that is usually a sure indication that she is guilty and trying to make you feel bad because of it.<P>Sorry for being callous but facts are facts and some people don't deserve happiness when they are willing to dish out pain so readily to others to get it.<P>It will take time and you will get over it. Battle on buddy and start your new life now, don't waste years because she has been too immature to work on her marriage.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000118||Broken_hearted||07-18-2001||11:31 PM||||OK, maybe I have been a little biased on what I post here. Jennifer is ripping my heart out right now and it hurts like hell. She was the best thing that ever happened to me and I ruined it. Now don't go patting me on the back and tell me it's not my fault. I know it's not all my fault, but it mostly is.<P>In the beginning, I was the sweetest guy (she told me this, but I just figured she was just saying that). When we were dating, she was feeling kind of sick when I called her at her apartment. I talked to her for a bit and then asked if I could come over and take care of her, and I did. Another time she was at my house when she was feeling kind of sick. I didn't know what to do, so I went to a guy's house that was selling puppies. I was living with my family at the time and knew I couldn't have a dog. The dog was an American Eskimo - Jennifer's favorite type of dog. I gave the guy 175 bucks(asking price) to let me 'borrow' it for a few hours. I didn't tell Jennifer where I was going, and when I returned, I had this puppy in my arms. She cheered up right away. I did things like this because I wanted to see her smile, and her to be happy. I did tons of things like this all the time - it's the way I was.<P>Then we got married. I guess the little things I did kind of faded away. I guess I'm the type of guy that brings stress home with me and it piles up until it spills out... Jennifer was usually there to catch it. I know I could be difficult at times, but I never just started in on her and just kept after her. But I did vent my frustrations to her, and it usually came out on her. Then one day I was very mad about something that had nothing to do with her, and I put the final nail in my coffin. This was about 3 years ago (maybe longer) when I told her I never wanted kids. That's the reason this is happening. I wasn't serious, but I had no right to tell her that, and it wasn't the truth. I didn't tell her I was sorry - I just let it go not wanting to remind her of it. Over the years I did little things that let her know I did want kids, I even told her I was ready for kids before she even started talking to this guy. Once I even told her that was the type of vehicle we would have to haul our kids around in instead of a minivan (pointing to a huge 4 door truck). I even told her we would probably have twins because it runs in my family (grandpa and his brother are twins). I am not sure if it's the truth, but that is the biggest reason she wants a divorce, and she doesn't believe me when I tell her I do want kids. You ever wish you could go back to a certain point in time?<P>Maybe I am just feeling sorry for myself here, but I am putting all my cards on the table. I never had a lot of money... I have a job that makes me work my butt of for a descent amount of money, but not enough. I know money shouldn't be a reason for her leaving, but it is. One time I bought a truck after mine was stolen. Turns out that truck had a salvage title, and when I traded it in on the truck I have now, the dealer sued me for $3,000. I didn't know the truck had a salvage title and the bank wouldn't have loaned on it, but they did. I sued the dealer that sold it to me and lost (long story stort: he had a copy of my signature on a paper stating I knew it was a salvage vehicle). There it was... credit was shot. We ended up borrowing money from her parents to pay the judgement... another mistake. We were never poor and have a descent life (especially after she found a good job, but even that is only seasonal), but I guess the fact that I have the newer truck (needed a good truck to haul tools around - I work construction) and she has the old car. <P>Then there is the thing with her family. Her family was always having reunions and stuff like that. I usually decined to go because I just don't like that kind of thing. I know I should have made more of an effort to do things she wanted to do, but laying on the couch watching TV just seemed to be what I wanted to do. She has a huge family and I know hardly any of them. When I did see them, like at weddings or funerals, they never really wanted to speak to me. Once Jennifer left me all alone and nobody talked to me. I just felt isolated in this huge crowd... it was usually like that. I did go with her to see her parents and grandparents because I kind of knew them. I didn't see them as much as I should have. We went to her parents house every Christmas and I am going to miss that.<P>I could go on and on but it comes down to this: I used to be the guy she wanted, but not anymore. She stopped loving me slowly over the years, and even though the old me is back, it's too late (guess why). I sit here and think back and can't believe she wants to leave... it was great in the beginning, but now I guess it's just boring. I do things that are exciting, but she never expressed interest. Now she tells me she always wanted to go with me, but I never asked her to go. After asking her several times if she wanted to go and her being not interested, I stopped asking - I just told her where I was going - maybe she should have asked to go with me?<P>It started out like a fairy tale. ||||reg||1
Z||000119||JSocha||07-19-2001||09:21 AM||||Broken Hearted:  For future reference when you meet another lady and the stresses of life are taking their toll.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><B>The Trouble Tree</B><P>The carpenter I hired to help me restore an old farmhouse had just finished a rough first day on the job.  A flat tire made him lose an hour of work, his electric saw quit working, and now his ancient pickup truck refused to start.  While I drove him home, he sat in stony silence.  On arriving, he invited me in to meet his family.  As we walked toward the front door, he paused briefly at a small tree, touching the tips of the branches with both hands.<P>When opening the door he underwent an amazing transformation.  His tanned face was wreathed in smiles and he hugged his two small children and gave his wife a kiss.  Afterward he walked me to the car.  We passed the tree and my curiosity got the better of me.  I asked him about what I had seen him do earlier.<P>"Oh, that's my trouble tree," he replied. "I know I can't help having troubles on the job, but one thing is for sure, troubles don't belong in the house with my wife and the children.  So I just hang them up on the tree every night when I come home.  Then in the morning I pick them up again."<P>"Funny thing is," he smiled, "when I come out in the morning to pick 'em up, there aren't nearly as many as I remember hanging up the night before."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P><I>Author unknown</I><P>Just a little "Insiprational" thought I felt I would share with you.  We all go through days where we bring our problems via stress home, do and/or say things we don't truely mean.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
